{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019etuVoUy2CHaE9x6qVGFgm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR; long paper 8-page limit + unlimited refs)\n5\t- Page limit: 8 pages main text (当前 10 页，需压缩 2 页)\n6\t\n7\t## Paper Type\n8\tEmpirical analysis / psychometric characterization. Discovery paper, not methods paper. \"DIF reveals benchmark structural problems,\" not \"DIF detects contamination.\"\n9\t\n10\t## Core Claims (4 条, 可证伪)\n11\t\n12\t**Claim 1 (Temporal Comparability Concern)**: ~31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations — they exhibit large DIF (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. Primary estimate: **31.3%** (temporal cohort, plan_001). All 57 subjects show instability (C% range 20.7–48.4%, unweighted mean ≈30%, plan_001 domain_specificity.csv). Within-subject matching on 5 high-C% subjects yields higher rates (31-45%, avg 38.1%, plan_014) — report as robustness check, not primary. This instability is not a statistical artifact (random split sanity check: 0.18%), and correlates weakly with domain-specific accuracy improvement (ρ=0.307, p=0.02), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains only 2.4pp (7.6%). Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), excluding the 1 non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Threshold sensitivity robustness confirmed: C% ranges from 48.9% (|Δ_MH|≥1.0) to 18.3% (|Δ_MH|≥2.0) to 10.2% (|Δ_MH|≥2.5). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 has negligible impact (removes 3/3,918 items).\n13\t- Evidence: plan_001 (C%=31.3%), plan_014 (within-subject C%=38.1%), plan_010 (arch-stratified: 99.98% decoder-only, C% unchanged, Jaccard=1.0), plan_018 (threshold: 49/31/18/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5; BH negligible), plan_019 (decomposition: ≤2.4pp/7.6% explained by domain improvement), plan_022 (model independence: graduated de-duplication to 1 model per family → C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap K; K=20: 29.1% vs random 30.9%, K=5: 11.7% vs random 16.9%; family structure does NOT inflate C%), plan_027 (temporal ability overlap: d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%; 1:1 NN matching C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp; contrast with Llama-2v3 d=-0.99→matched C%=28.3%, where ability confound explains 33pp)\n14\t- Honesty requirement: ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) at subject level reflects a modest ecological correlation. Item-level decomposition (plan_019) shows domain improvement accounts for at most 2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific. Caveat: item difficulty may be a mediator rather than confounder — use Model 1 as primary; Model 2 (Simpson's paradox) as supplementary.\n15\t- IRT fit: 98% of models show |Lz|>2, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity. 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU — discuss in limitations as motivation for future multidimensional IRT work.\n16\t\n17\t**Claim 2 (Ground Truth Meta-critique)**: Existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al. labels is expected, not failure — these labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This is a fundamental validation methodology gap: all work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind spot. **Empirically validated** (plan_026): direct comparison of DIF-C flags vs Li et al. web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items shows enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, Phi=0.012, χ²=1.28 (p=0.258), OR=1.05 [0.96, 1.15]. Consistent across subtypes (input_only: 0.986, input_and_label: 0.960). Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF can detect differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0).\n18\t- Evidence: plan_001 (enrichment=0.96), plan_026 (direct comparison: enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, p=0.258), plan_011 (ΔTPR=+0.508, dose-response)\n19\t- Framing: aggressive — \"the field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection\" — now with direct empirical evidence, not just conceptual argument\n20\t\n21\t**Claim 3 (Semi-synthetic Validation Framework)**: The ΔTPR methodology — injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring detection power increase — provides a controlled validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector. Cross-subject matching (using total score on OTHER subjects as MH matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\n22\t- Evidence: plan_011, plan_016 (ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject, 4/5 subjects ΔTPR>0.3), plan_004 Step 2 (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818 standard, +0.517 external)\n23\t- Cross-benchmark: GSM8K replication confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks\n24\t- Positioning: cross-subject matching is a practical methodological recommendation; cross-benchmark replication strengthens generalizability\n25\t\n26\t**Claim 4 (Downstream Impact — Four-Layer Finding)**: DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter. Four sub-findings:\n27\t1. **Benchmark redundancy**: ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings — MMLU rankings are extremely robust to 31% item removal, revealing massive measurement redundancy in the benchmark.\n28\t2. **DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are NOT the most ranking-defining items**: DIF removal causes MORE disruption than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ, 21/21 conditions), confirming DIF items are non-trivial. But circularity ablation (plan_020) shows DIF is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10-16× more (z=-157 vs -9.5), high-variance 11× more (z=-108), high-discrimination 1.7× more (z=-16). MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1% (Jaccard=0.12) — DIF identifies \"items that changed across generations,\" not \"good items.\"\n29\t3. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF identifies which items' functioning changes most across model generations → reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts. Not \"find bad items and remove\" but \"understand what changed and why.\" Model-specific patterns: phi-3/llama-3 gain +700-850 ranks from DIF items, mistral merges lose -400 to -587. **Item characterization** (plan_023): DIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's d=-0.247) and higher-discrimination (d=0.230) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains (χ² p=0.32). Key finding: difficulty × DIF direction interaction (ρ=-0.597) — hardest-decile DIF-C items are 88.7% focal-favoring (2024 models do better), easiest-decile are 96.2% reference-favoring (2023 models do better). This reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.\n30\t4. **Cross-benchmark validation — DIF items resist contamination-based gains**: On GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, p=0.005, 95% BCa CI [−0.714, −0.155]; Spearman ρ=−0.403, p=0.020, CI [−0.687, −0.031]; partial r=−0.385 controlling GSM8K accuracy, p=0.030, CI [−0.640, −0.058]; permutation p=0.014). All CIs exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps show disproportionately lower performance on DIF-C items. This is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items, while DIF items capture genuine capability differences.\n31\t- Evidence: plan_017 (z=-8 to -49σ, ρ=0.996-0.999), plan_020 (circularity ablation: DIF z=-9.5 vs acc-gap z=-157, disc z=-16, var z=-108), plan_004 Step 3 (r=−0.478, p=0.005, N=33), plan_023 (item characterization: difficulty ρ=-0.597, domain uniformity χ² p=0.32)\n32\t- Architecture: plan_017 is load-bearing (strong, full N=5,227), Step 3 adds contamination dimension but is suggestive (N=33, CI wide). If Step 3 attacked, plan_017 stands independently.\n33\t- Growing Pains互补: \"Growing Pains uses anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic tells them which anchor items are degrading\"\n34\t- Caveats (→ limitations): N=33, Pearson CI upper bound |r|=0.155 (small effect possible), Spearman CI upper bound |ρ|=0.031 (near zero possible); item difficulty confound not fully excluded (DIF-C items harder for all models); contamination gap is proxy, not ground truth; replication with larger matched sample needed\n35\t\n36\t## Story Spine (v2 — no-pivot narrative)\n37\t\n38\t**Opening frame**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct. But do they? Psychometrics has a mature tool for testing exactly this: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items) — the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\n39\t\n40\t**Key narrative principle**: \"Instability ≠ invalidity.\" DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations — this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant (ρ≈0.997 after removing 31% of items), so instability doesn't threaten validity. But it reveals WHICH capability dimensions are shifting. **This framing must appear in the Introduction, not be deferred to Discussion.**\n41\t\n42\t**What we find** (4 contributions, focused):\n43\t\n44\t1. **31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable** across model generations (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5). Robust to threshold choice (49%/31%/18%/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3918 items), and architecture composition (99.98% decoder-only). Domain accuracy improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific, not a proxy for \"models got better.\"\n45\t\n46\t2. **Ground truth meta-critique**: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment≈1.0 is expected. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF detects differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508). Any item-level detector validated against web-overlap labels has this blind spot.\n47\t\n48\t3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework**: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). Portable across benchmarks (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818). Reusable for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n49\t\n50\t4. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal (z=-8 to -49σ vs random) but are the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy (z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157). DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just informative items. Cross-benchmark: DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478, BCa CI [−0.71, −0.16]).\n51\t\n52\t**Headline result**: \"Psychometric DIF analysis of 5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items reveals 31% temporal instability — robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and not a validity threat (ρ≈0.997 ranking stability). The field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; we provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478) and serve as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations.\"\n53\t\n54\t## Scope Control (main body vs appendix)\n55\t\n56\t**MAIN BODY (4 claims + robustness)**:\n57\t- §1 Introduction (problem + \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing upfront)\n58\t- §2 Related Work (45 papers, 5 clusters)\n59\t- §3 Method (MH-DIF primer, semi-synthetic framework, cross-subject matching)\n60\t- §4 Experiments: Claim 1 (temporal DIF + threshold + BH + decomposition + architecture), Claim 2 (enrichment + meta-critique), Claim 3 (semi-synthetic + cross-benchmark), Claim 4 (redundancy + circularity ablation + diagnostic lens + GSM1K validation)\n61\t- §5 Discussion + Limitations\n62\t- §6 Conclusion\n63\t\n64\t**APPENDIX (pruned from main body)**:\n65\t- Bayesian cumulative DIF (plan_007) — simulation-only, underdeveloped\n66\t- RL robustness (plan_002) — N=13, underpowered\n67\t- Ensemble fusion (plan_003a/b/c) — negative, not executed\n68\t- Bifactor equating pilot — below target fit\n69\t- Item generation pilot — below benchmarks\n70\t- Graceful degradation — clean but supplementary\n71\t- Item-Focused Trees — robustness check, supplementary\n72\t- ConStat/DVD comparisons — complementary baselines\n73\t\n74\t## Empirical Observation: Structural Constraints on DIF in LLM Benchmarks\n75\t\n76\tSystematic experimentation reveals that three structural improvements — matched-ability cohort design (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012, r=0.9986), and bifactor pre-check (plan_014, within-subject C%=38.1%) — all fail to reduce temporal DIF baseline FPR below 31% while maintaining detection power. This is an empirical observation, not a formal impossibility result. The constraints arise from: (a) unidimensional IRT limitations in modeling 57-subject multidimensional ability, (b) statistical power requirements (N≥80, δ≥1.4), and (c) inability to separate ability confounding from genuine item-level changes. Higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints — future work.\n77\t\n78\t## Venue Fit\n79\t\n80\t- **EMNLP 2026**: Strong fit — NLP evaluation methodology is a dedicated area, reviewers expect empirical studies of benchmarks. \"Empirical, not algorithmic\" contribution type well-received. Risk: 8-page limit requires significant compression (current 10 pages). Mitigation: move structural analysis details to appendix.\n81\t\n82\t## Novelty Articulation (三层，显式化)\n83\t\n84\tMH-DIF (1959/1988) is applied without modification. **Explicitly acknowledge this.** Novelty is NOT algorithmic. Three layers of genuine contribution:\n85\t\n86\t1. **Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale**: First DIF analysis on 5,227×12,508 LLM response matrix. Finding: 31% temporal instability is robust across thresholds (10-49%), not explained by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98% decoder-only). No prior work has characterized item-level stability of LLM benchmarks at this scale. The instability finding itself is new data about MMLU's measurement properties.\n87\t\n88\t2. **Ground truth meta-critique with field-wide implications**: The observation that web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors is NOT obvious — the field routinely uses them. This affects the validity claims of Min-K%, ConStat, n-gram methods, and all papers using web-overlap as ground truth. First empirical demonstration (enrichment≈1.0 + semi-synthetic proof).\n89\t\n90\t3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework + cross-subject matching**: The ΔTPR methodology is a reusable infrastructure contribution. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a novel practical innovation — the insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle and general. Portable to any multi-domain benchmark (demonstrated on GSM8K).\n91\t\n92\t**Framing**: \"We bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework — not a new algorithm.\"\n93\t\n94\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns (updated for pivot)\n95\t\n96\t1. **\"31% could just mean models got better / ability confound inflates C%\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_019 + plan_027). Item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). Temporal cohort ability gap is negligible (d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%); 1:1 NN matching (d≈-0.01) yields C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp. Contrast: Llama-2v3 (d=-0.99) drops from 61.3% to 28.3% under matching. Both converge to ~28-29% genuine item-level DIF.\n97\t\n98\t2. **\"Ground truth critique is obvious — of course web labels don't capture differential effects\"** — Not obvious: the field routinely uses these labels for item-level validation. We provide the first empirical demonstration with both the enrichment≈1.0 result and the semi-synthetic proof of concept.\n99\t\n100\t3. **\"Semi-synthetic injection is artificial\"** — We inject into real response matrices (not simulated), preserving real ability distributions and item characteristics. The mechanism (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification acknowledged in limitations.\n101\t\n102\t4. **\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects\"** — Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most. Operational region: N≥80, δ≥1.4, TPR=0.68-0.80.\n103\t\n104\t5. **\"Only validated on MMLU\"** — Partially addressed: GSM8K replication (METABENCH 1,319 items × 6,702 models) confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (ΔTPR=+0.818) and temporal DIF pattern (C%=18.9%). GSM1K contamination gap validation (N=33) provides cross-benchmark evidence. Full generalization to diverse benchmarks remains future work.\n105\t\n106\t6. **\"Higher methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) could solve the confounding\"** — Acknowledged as future work. We frame our three-attempt negative results as \"empirical observation\" that motivates these directions, not as impossibility.\n107\t\n108\t7. **\"ETS threshold is uncalibrated for LLM populations\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). C% at {1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5} = {48.9%, 31.3%, 18.3%, 10.2%}. Finding >15% holds at standard thresholds [1.0, 2.0]. At conservative 2.5, still 10.2% (1,278 items). Present as: \"finding is robust across standard thresholds; even at the most conservative cutoff, >10% of items show large DIF.\" Do NOT claim \">15% at all thresholds\" — be honest that 2.5 drops below.\n109\t\n110\t8. **\"Multiple testing: 12,508 simultaneous tests without correction\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 removes only 3/3,918 ETS C items (31.32%→31.30%). At higher thresholds, BH impact is zero. ETS dual requirement (effect size + significance) provides near-complete implicit protection. Random-split sanity check (C%=0.18%) confirms empirically.\n111\t\n112\t9. **\"Only uniform DIF — non-uniform DIF plausible for LLMs\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_021). LR-DIF on 9 subjects (1787 items): MH C-class 92.1% concordance with LR uniform DIF → MH detection robust. Non-uniform DIF: 38.7% at significance threshold, **24.8% at |β_interaction|≥0.5** (conservative, effect-size controlled). MH structurally underestimates instability. Caveat: LR used within-subject matching (vs MH's cross-benchmark), so 38.7% may be inflated; use 24.8% in paper. Framing: \"our 31% uniform-only estimate is conservative.\"\n113\t\n114\t10. **\"5,227 models are not independent — fine-tunes/merges inflate N and DIF significance\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_022). Graduated de-duplication (capping models per family at K=1,2,5,10,20) shows C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap: K=20 (447 models) C%=29.1% vs random 30.9%; K=5 (146 models) C%=11.7% vs random 16.9%; K=1 (33 families) C%=0.17% (underpowered, N<80). Family structure does NOT inflate C% — if anything, de-duplication reduces it slightly less than random subsampling. The 31.3% figure is robust to model non-independence.\n115\t\n116\t11. **\"Ranking disruption result is tautological\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_020). Circularity ablation shows DIF removal is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157, discrimination z=-16, variance z=-108. Despite 62.6% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning makes DIF 10-16× less disruptive. DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just items with large raw group differences. Tautology refuted.\n117\t\n118\t## Dropped from Original Framing\n119\t\n120\t- **Ensemble (Claim 2 old)**: plan_003a/b/c never executed. Future work.\n121\t- **Regeneration (Claim 3 old)**: Bifactor killed by plan_014 pre-check. Dropped entirely.\n122\t- **RL robustness**: Theoretically motivated but N=13 insufficient. Limitations disclosure, not claim.\n123\t- **Detection as primary contribution**: Pivoted from \"we detect contamination\" to \"we characterize benchmark properties.\"\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c61f7179-56df-476f-b574-625c8ff520d1","uuid":"b73d1706-9925-4823-a7eb-b84df6479d50","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:15:55.683Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR; long paper 8-page limit + unlimited refs)\n- Page limit: 8 pages main text (当前 10 页，需压缩 2 页)\n\n## Paper Type\nEmpirical analysis / psychometric characterization. Discovery paper, not methods paper. \"DIF reveals benchmark structural problems,\" not \"DIF detects contamination.\"\n\n## Core Claims (4 条, 可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 (Temporal Comparability Concern)**: ~31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations — they exhibit large DIF (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. Primary estimate: **31.3%** (temporal cohort, plan_001). All 57 subjects show instability (C% range 20.7–48.4%, unweighted mean ≈30%, plan_001 domain_specificity.csv). Within-subject matching on 5 high-C% subjects yields higher rates (31-45%, avg 38.1%, plan_014) — report as robustness check, not primary. This instability is not a statistical artifact (random split sanity check: 0.18%), and correlates weakly with domain-specific accuracy improvement (ρ=0.307, p=0.02), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains only 2.4pp (7.6%). Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), excluding the 1 non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Threshold sensitivity robustness confirmed: C% ranges from 48.9% (|Δ_MH|≥1.0) to 18.3% (|Δ_MH|≥2.0) to 10.2% (|Δ_MH|≥2.5). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 has negligible impact (removes 3/3,918 items).\n- Evidence: plan_001 (C%=31.3%), plan_014 (within-subject C%=38.1%), plan_010 (arch-stratified: 99.98% decoder-only, C% unchanged, Jaccard=1.0), plan_018 (threshold: 49/31/18/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5; BH negligible), plan_019 (decomposition: ≤2.4pp/7.6% explained by domain improvement), plan_022 (model independence: graduated de-duplication to 1 model per family → C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap K; K=20: 29.1% vs random 30.9%, K=5: 11.7% vs random 16.9%; family structure does NOT inflate C%), plan_027 (temporal ability overlap: d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%; 1:1 NN matching C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp; contrast with Llama-2v3 d=-0.99→matched C%=28.3%, where ability confound explains 33pp)\n- Honesty requirement: ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) at subject level reflects a modest ecological correlation. Item-level decomposition (plan_019) shows domain improvement accounts for at most 2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific. Caveat: item difficulty may be a mediator rather than confounder — use Model 1 as primary; Model 2 (Simpson's paradox) as supplementary.\n- IRT fit: 98% of models show |Lz|>2, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity. 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU — discuss in limitations as motivation for future multidimensional IRT work.\n\n**Claim 2 (Ground Truth Meta-critique)**: Existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al. labels is expected, not failure — these labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This is a fundamental validation methodology gap: all work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind spot. **Empirically validated** (plan_026): direct comparison of DIF-C flags vs Li et al. web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items shows enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, Phi=0.012, χ²=1.28 (p=0.258), OR=1.05 [0.96, 1.15]. Consistent across subtypes (input_only: 0.986, input_and_label: 0.960). Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF can detect differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0).\n- Evidence: plan_001 (enrichment=0.96), plan_026 (direct comparison: enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, p=0.258), plan_011 (ΔTPR=+0.508, dose-response)\n- Framing: aggressive — \"the field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection\" — now with direct empirical evidence, not just conceptual argument\n\n**Claim 3 (Semi-synthetic Validation Framework)**: The ΔTPR methodology — injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring detection power increase — provides a controlled validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector. Cross-subject matching (using total score on OTHER subjects as MH matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\n- Evidence: plan_011, plan_016 (ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject, 4/5 subjects ΔTPR>0.3), plan_004 Step 2 (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818 standard, +0.517 external)\n- Cross-benchmark: GSM8K replication confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks\n- Positioning: cross-subject matching is a practical methodological recommendation; cross-benchmark replication strengthens generalizability\n\n**Claim 4 (Downstream Impact — Four-Layer Finding)**: DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter. Four sub-findings:\n1. **Benchmark redundancy**: ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings — MMLU rankings are extremely robust to 31% item removal, revealing massive measurement redundancy in the benchmark.\n2. **DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are NOT the most ranking-defining items**: DIF removal causes MORE disruption than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ, 21/21 conditions), confirming DIF items are non-trivial. But circularity ablation (plan_020) shows DIF is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10-16× more (z=-157 vs -9.5), high-variance 11× more (z=-108), high-discrimination 1.7× more (z=-16). MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1% (Jaccard=0.12) — DIF identifies \"items that changed across generations,\" not \"good items.\"\n3. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF identifies which items' functioning changes most across model generations → reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts. Not \"find bad items and remove\" but \"understand what changed and why.\" Model-specific patterns: phi-3/llama-3 gain +700-850 ranks from DIF items, mistral merges lose -400 to -587. **Item characterization** (plan_023): DIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's d=-0.247) and higher-discrimination (d=0.230) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains (χ² p=0.32). Key finding: difficulty × DIF direction interaction (ρ=-0.597) — hardest-decile DIF-C items are 88.7% focal-favoring (2024 models do better), easiest-decile are 96.2% reference-favoring (2023 models do better). This reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.\n4. **Cross-benchmark validation — DIF items resist contamination-based gains**: On GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, p=0.005, 95% BCa CI [−0.714, −0.155]; Spearman ρ=−0.403, p=0.020, CI [−0.687, −0.031]; partial r=−0.385 controlling GSM8K accuracy, p=0.030, CI [−0.640, −0.058]; permutation p=0.014). All CIs exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps show disproportionately lower performance on DIF-C items. This is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items, while DIF items capture genuine capability differences.\n- Evidence: plan_017 (z=-8 to -49σ, ρ=0.996-0.999), plan_020 (circularity ablation: DIF z=-9.5 vs acc-gap z=-157, disc z=-16, var z=-108), plan_004 Step 3 (r=−0.478, p=0.005, N=33), plan_023 (item characterization: difficulty ρ=-0.597, domain uniformity χ² p=0.32)\n- Architecture: plan_017 is load-bearing (strong, full N=5,227), Step 3 adds contamination dimension but is suggestive (N=33, CI wide). If Step 3 attacked, plan_017 stands independently.\n- Growing Pains互补: \"Growing Pains uses anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic tells them which anchor items are degrading\"\n- Caveats (→ limitations): N=33, Pearson CI upper bound |r|=0.155 (small effect possible), Spearman CI upper bound |ρ|=0.031 (near zero possible); item difficulty confound not fully excluded (DIF-C items harder for all models); contamination gap is proxy, not ground truth; replication with larger matched sample needed\n\n## Story Spine (v2 — no-pivot narrative)\n\n**Opening frame**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct. But do they? Psychometrics has a mature tool for testing exactly this: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items) — the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\n\n**Key narrative principle**: \"Instability ≠ invalidity.\" DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations — this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant (ρ≈0.997 after removing 31% of items), so instability doesn't threaten validity. But it reveals WHICH capability dimensions are shifting. **This framing must appear in the Introduction, not be deferred to Discussion.**\n\n**What we find** (4 contributions, focused):\n\n1. **31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable** across model generations (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5). Robust to threshold choice (49%/31%/18%/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3918 items), and architecture composition (99.98% decoder-only). Domain accuracy improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific, not a proxy for \"models got better.\"\n\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique**: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment≈1.0 is expected. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF detects differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508). Any item-level detector validated against web-overlap labels has this blind spot.\n\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework**: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). Portable across benchmarks (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818). Reusable for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n\n4. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal (z=-8 to -49σ vs random) but are the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy (z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157). DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just informative items. Cross-benchmark: DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478, BCa CI [−0.71, −0.16]).\n\n**Headline result**: \"Psychometric DIF analysis of 5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items reveals 31% temporal instability — robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and not a validity threat (ρ≈0.997 ranking stability). The field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; we provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478) and serve as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations.\"\n\n## Scope Control (main body vs appendix)\n\n**MAIN BODY (4 claims + robustness)**:\n- §1 Introduction (problem + \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing upfront)\n- §2 Related Work (45 papers, 5 clusters)\n- §3 Method (MH-DIF primer, semi-synthetic framework, cross-subject matching)\n- §4 Experiments: Claim 1 (temporal DIF + threshold + BH + decomposition + architecture), Claim 2 (enrichment + meta-critique), Claim 3 (semi-synthetic + cross-benchmark), Claim 4 (redundancy + circularity ablation + diagnostic lens + GSM1K validation)\n- §5 Discussion + Limitations\n- §6 Conclusion\n\n**APPENDIX (pruned from main body)**:\n- Bayesian cumulative DIF (plan_007) — simulation-only, underdeveloped\n- RL robustness (plan_002) — N=13, underpowered\n- Ensemble fusion (plan_003a/b/c) — negative, not executed\n- Bifactor equating pilot — below target fit\n- Item generation pilot — below benchmarks\n- Graceful degradation — clean but supplementary\n- Item-Focused Trees — robustness check, supplementary\n- ConStat/DVD comparisons — complementary baselines\n\n## Empirical Observation: Structural Constraints on DIF in LLM Benchmarks\n\nSystematic experimentation reveals that three structural improvements — matched-ability cohort design (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012, r=0.9986), and bifactor pre-check (plan_014, within-subject C%=38.1%) — all fail to reduce temporal DIF baseline FPR below 31% while maintaining detection power. This is an empirical observation, not a formal impossibility result. The constraints arise from: (a) unidimensional IRT limitations in modeling 57-subject multidimensional ability, (b) statistical power requirements (N≥80, δ≥1.4), and (c) inability to separate ability confounding from genuine item-level changes. Higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints — future work.\n\n## Venue Fit\n\n- **EMNLP 2026**: Strong fit — NLP evaluation methodology is a dedicated area, reviewers expect empirical studies of benchmarks. \"Empirical, not algorithmic\" contribution type well-received. Risk: 8-page limit requires significant compression (current 10 pages). Mitigation: move structural analysis details to appendix.\n\n## Novelty Articulation (三层，显式化)\n\nMH-DIF (1959/1988) is applied without modification. **Explicitly acknowledge this.** Novelty is NOT algorithmic. Three layers of genuine contribution:\n\n1. **Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale**: First DIF analysis on 5,227×12,508 LLM response matrix. Finding: 31% temporal instability is robust across thresholds (10-49%), not explained by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98% decoder-only). No prior work has characterized item-level stability of LLM benchmarks at this scale. The instability finding itself is new data about MMLU's measurement properties.\n\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique with field-wide implications**: The observation that web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors is NOT obvious — the field routinely uses them. This affects the validity claims of Min-K%, ConStat, n-gram methods, and all papers using web-overlap as ground truth. First empirical demonstration (enrichment≈1.0 + semi-synthetic proof).\n\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework + cross-subject matching**: The ΔTPR methodology is a reusable infrastructure contribution. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a novel practical innovation — the insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle and general. Portable to any multi-domain benchmark (demonstrated on GSM8K).\n\n**Framing**: \"We bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework — not a new algorithm.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns (updated for pivot)\n\n1. **\"31% could just mean models got better / ability confound inflates C%\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_019 + plan_027). Item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). Temporal cohort ability gap is negligible (d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%); 1:1 NN matching (d≈-0.01) yields C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp. Contrast: Llama-2v3 (d=-0.99) drops from 61.3% to 28.3% under matching. Both converge to ~28-29% genuine item-level DIF.\n\n2. **\"Ground truth critique is obvious — of course web labels don't capture differential effects\"** — Not obvious: the field routinely uses these labels for item-level validation. We provide the first empirical demonstration with both the enrichment≈1.0 result and the semi-synthetic proof of concept.\n\n3. **\"Semi-synthetic injection is artificial\"** — We inject into real response matrices (not simulated), preserving real ability distributions and item characteristics. The mechanism (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification acknowledged in limitations.\n\n4. **\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects\"** — Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most. Operational region: N≥80, δ≥1.4, TPR=0.68-0.80.\n\n5. **\"Only validated on MMLU\"** — Partially addressed: GSM8K replication (METABENCH 1,319 items × 6,702 models) confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (ΔTPR=+0.818) and temporal DIF pattern (C%=18.9%). GSM1K contamination gap validation (N=33) provides cross-benchmark evidence. Full generalization to diverse benchmarks remains future work.\n\n6. **\"Higher methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) could solve the confounding\"** — Acknowledged as future work. We frame our three-attempt negative results as \"empirical observation\" that motivates these directions, not as impossibility.\n\n7. **\"ETS threshold is uncalibrated for LLM populations\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). C% at {1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5} = {48.9%, 31.3%, 18.3%, 10.2%}. Finding >15% holds at standard thresholds [1.0, 2.0]. At conservative 2.5, still 10.2% (1,278 items). Present as: \"finding is robust across standard thresholds; even at the most conservative cutoff, >10% of items show large DIF.\" Do NOT claim \">15% at all thresholds\" — be honest that 2.5 drops below.\n\n8. **\"Multiple testing: 12,508 simultaneous tests without correction\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 removes only 3/3,918 ETS C items (31.32%→31.30%). At higher thresholds, BH impact is zero. ETS dual requirement (effect size + significance) provides near-complete implicit protection. Random-split sanity check (C%=0.18%) confirms empirically.\n\n9. **\"Only uniform DIF — non-uniform DIF plausible for LLMs\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_021). LR-DIF on 9 subjects (1787 items): MH C-class 92.1% concordance with LR uniform DIF → MH detection robust. Non-uniform DIF: 38.7% at significance threshold, **24.8% at |β_interaction|≥0.5** (conservative, effect-size controlled). MH structurally underestimates instability. Caveat: LR used within-subject matching (vs MH's cross-benchmark), so 38.7% may be inflated; use 24.8% in paper. Framing: \"our 31% uniform-only estimate is conservative.\"\n\n10. **\"5,227 models are not independent — fine-tunes/merges inflate N and DIF significance\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_022). Graduated de-duplication (capping models per family at K=1,2,5,10,20) shows C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap: K=20 (447 models) C%=29.1% vs random 30.9%; K=5 (146 models) C%=11.7% vs random 16.9%; K=1 (33 families) C%=0.17% (underpowered, N<80). Family structure does NOT inflate C% — if anything, de-duplication reduces it slightly less than random subsampling. The 31.3% figure is robust to model non-independence.\n\n11. **\"Ranking disruption result is tautological\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_020). Circularity ablation shows DIF removal is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157, discrimination z=-16, variance z=-108. Despite 62.6% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning makes DIF 10-16× less disruptive. DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just items with large raw group differences. Tautology refuted.\n\n## Dropped from Original Framing\n\n- **Ensemble (Claim 2 old)**: plan_003a/b/c never executed. Future work.\n- **Regeneration (Claim 3 old)**: Bifactor killed by plan_014 pre-check. Dropped entirely.\n- **RL robustness**: Theoretically motivated but N=13 insufficient. Limitations disclosure, not claim.\n- **Detection as primary contribution**: Pivoted from \"we detect contamination\" to \"we characterize benchmark properties.\"\n","numLines":124,"startLine":1,"totalLines":124}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JzkGDujWkNJY16uHFJSR3S","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"Apply MH-DIF to full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models × 12,508 items). Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, architecture stratification, within-family confound control (Llama-2 vs Llama-3), domain-specificity analysis, and proxy validation against Li et al. web-overlap labels.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n7\t      \"temporal_enrichment_vs_li\": 0.96,\n8\t      \"temporal_fpr_on_li_clean\": 0.318,\n9\t      \"ability_quintile_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n10\t      \"ability_quintile_enrichment\": 1.19,\n11\t      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n12\t      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n13\t      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n14\t      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n15\t      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n16\t      \"temporal_ability_jaccard\": 0.014,\n17\t      \"n_subjects_with_dif\": 57,\n18\t      \"subject_c_pct_range\": \"20.7–48.4\",\n19\t      \"subject_c_pct_unweighted_mean\": 30.0\n20\t    },\n21\t    \"tables\": [\n22\t      {\n23\t        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF Detection Summary by Cohort Definition\",\n24\t        \"columns\": [\n25\t          \"Cohort\",\n26\t          \"N_ref\",\n27\t          \"N_foc\",\n28\t          \"ETS_A%\",\n29\t          \"ETS_B%\",\n30\t          \"ETS_C%\",\n31\t          \"Enrichment\"\n32\t        ],\n33\t        \"rows\": [\n34\t          [\n35\t            \"Temporal (2023 vs 2024-H1)\",\n36\t            1253,\n37\t            656,\n38\t            \"47.8\",\n39\t            \"20.9\",\n40\t            \"31.3\",\n41\t            0.96\n42\t          ],\n43\t          [\n44\t            \"Ability (Q1 vs Q5)\",\n45\t            1044,\n46\t            1044,\n47\t            \"89.2\",\n48\t            \"7.9\",\n49\t            \"2.9\",\n50\t            1.19\n51\t          ],\n52\t          [\n53\t            \"Random split (sanity)\",\n54\t            960,\n55\t            960,\n56\t            \"97.5\",\n57\t            \"2.3\",\n58\t            \"0.18\",\n59\t            \"—\"\n60\t          ]\n61\t        ]\n62\t      },\n63\t      {\n64\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: Within-Family DIF Confound Control\",\n65\t        \"columns\": [\n66\t          \"Family pair\",\n67\t          \"N_ref\",\n68\t          \"N_foc\",\n69\t          \"C%\",\n70\t          \"Interpretation\"\n71\t        ],\n72\t        \"rows\": [\n73\t          [\n74\t            \"Llama-2 vs Llama-3\",\n75\t            161,\n76\t            308,\n77\t            \"61.3\",\n78\t            \"Capability gain + architecture shift\"\n79\t          ],\n80\t          [\n81\t            \"Mistral vs Mixtral\",\n82\t            134,\n83\t            89,\n84\t            \"38.7\",\n85\t            \"Architecture shift (dense→MoE)\"\n86\t          ]\n87\t        ]\n88\t      }\n89\t    ],\n90\t    \"figures\": [],\n91\t    \"analysis\": \"The 31.3% figure anchors the entire paper. Its robustness is demonstrated through three internal controls: (1) ability-only cohort produces 2.9% — proving temporal signal is not an ability artifact, (2) random split produces 0.18% — proving it is not statistical noise, (3) enrichment ≈ 1.0 — proving DIF flags are orthogonal to web-overlap contamination labels. The within-family results are higher than temporal (61.3% for Llama-2v3) because these compare families with known architecture changes, validating DIF sensitivity to genuine cross-generation shifts. In the best tier, reviewers see this not as \\\"DIF fails\\\" but as \\\"DIF correctly identifies that MMLU items function differently across model generations.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the temporal cohort analysis cleanly reveals 31.3% ETS-C items — a number that is striking enough to command attention but not so extreme as to suggest methodological artifact. The near-unity enrichment (0.96) against Li et al. labels is the keystone of the meta-critique: DIF flags are orthogonal to web-overlap labels, confirming that these measure fundamentally different constructs (differential vs global exposure). The ability-quintile cohort shows only 2.9% C-items — a critical internal control proving that when groups differ only in ability (not temporally), DIF correctly produces near-zero flags. The random-split sanity check at 0.18% eliminates statistical artifact concerns entirely. Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is high because these families have maximal temporal separation AND known architecture/training changes — this becomes a positive result showing DIF captures genuine cross-generation functional shifts. Domain ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) is modest but significant, appropriately handled by plan_019 decomposition showing domain improvement explains only 2.4pp. All 57 subjects showing some instability (range 20.7–48.4%) demonstrates this is a benchmark-wide phenomenon, not concentrated in a few pathological domains. Reviewers accept this as the definitive large-scale characterization of MMLU temporal stability.\\n\"\n92\t  },\n93\t  {\n94\t    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n95\t    \"description\": \"Validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models. SFT-only baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) mechanism control, and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO) focal models. Two-level validation: group-level DIF stability and model-level traditional detector degradation.\\n\",\n96\t    \"results\": {\n97\t      \"n_sft_baseline\": 312,\n98\t      \"n_alignment_rl\": 477,\n99\t      \"n_reasoning_rl\": 13,\n100\t      \"dif_c_pct_sft_cohort\": 30.8,\n101\t      \"dif_c_pct_alignment_rl_cohort\": 31.1,\n102\t      \"dif_c_pct_mixed_rl_cohort\": 30.5,\n103\t      \"jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl\": 0.87,\n104\t      \"jaccard_sft_vs_mixed_rl\": 0.84,\n105\t      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.72,\n106\t      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.41,\n107\t      \"mink_auroc_sft\": 0.68,\n108\t      \"mink_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.35,\n109\t      \"dif_retention_ratio_vs_constat\": 0.97,\n110\t      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.008,\n111\t      \"bootstrap_ci_dif_auroc\": \"[0.88, 0.94]\",\n112\t      \"delong_p_dif_vs_constat\": 0.003,\n113\t      \"partial_exploitation_boundary_p\": 0.45\n114\t    },\n115\t    \"tables\": [\n116\t      {\n117\t        \"name\": \"Table: DIF Stability Across Cohort Compositions\",\n118\t        \"columns\": [\n119\t          \"Cohort composition\",\n120\t          \"N_focal\",\n121\t          \"C%\",\n122\t          \"Jaccard vs SFT baseline\"\n123\t        ],\n124\t        \"rows\": [\n125\t          [\n126\t            \"SFT-only focal\",\n127\t            312,\n128\t            \"30.8\",\n129\t            \"1.00\"\n130\t          ],\n131\t          [\n132\t            \"+ Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO)\",\n133\t            789,\n134\t            \"31.1\",\n135\t            \"0.87\"\n136\t          ],\n137\t          [\n138\t            \"+ Reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\",\n139\t            802,\n140\t            \"30.5\",\n141\t            \"0.84\"\n142\t          ]\n143\t        ]\n144\t      },\n145\t      {\n146\t        \"name\": \"Table: Per-Model Detector Degradation on Reasoning-RL\",\n147\t        \"columns\": [\n148\t          \"Detector\",\n149\t          \"AUROC (SFT)\",\n150\t          \"AUROC (Reasoning-RL)\",\n151\t          \"Δ\",\n152\t          \"DeLong p\"\n153\t        ],\n154\t        \"rows\": [\n155\t          [\n156\t            \"DIF (group-level)\",\n157\t            \"0.91\",\n158\t            \"0.90\",\n159\t            \"-0.01\",\n160\t            \"0.82\"\n161\t          ],\n162\t          [\n163\t            \"ConStat\",\n164\t            \"0.72\",\n165\t            \"0.41\",\n166\t            \"-0.31\",\n167\t            \"0.003\"\n168\t          ],\n169\t          [\n170\t            \"Min-K%\",\n171\t            \"0.68\",\n172\t            \"0.35\",\n173\t            \"-0.33\",\n174\t            \"0.001\"\n175\t          ]\n176\t        ]\n177\t      }\n178\t    ],\n179\t    \"figures\": [],\n180\t    \"analysis\": \"This experiment serves a dual rhetorical purpose: (1) it shows DIF is robust to the specific threat model (RL concealing contamination signals) that motivates the paper, and (2) it provides a direct head-to-head comparison with existing per-model detectors. In the best tier, the N=13 limitation is acknowledged but reviewers accept the two-level design as sufficient — DIF's group-level nature means the validation logic is sound even with small RL samples. The partial exploitation analysis adds theoretical depth, characterizing when DIF works rather than just showing that it does.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, DIF's group-level nature makes it inherently robust to RL training: MH conditions on total score within the cohort, so individual model training methodology is irrelevant as long as the cohort composition doesn't systematically shift. With only 13 reasoning-RL models added to a focal group of 656+, the cohort composition barely changes (Jaccard 0.84 with baseline). The C% remains within 1pp of baseline across all three cohort compositions, confirming group-level stability. Meanwhile, ConStat and Min-K% — which operate at the individual model level — degrade sharply on reasoning-RL models (AUROC drops from 0.72/0.68 to 0.41/0.35) because GRPO/PPO flatten the NLL landscape these detectors rely on. TOST equivalence testing (p=0.008) formally confirms DIF retention is not just \\\"failed to reject null\\\" but positively equivalent. The partial exploitation analysis shows DIF detection boundary at p≈0.45, meaning contamination must affect <45% of focal group to evade — a reasonable operational bound. Reviewers accept the N=13 reasoning-RL sample because DIF's claim is group-level robustness (adding 13 models doesn't change group statistics), while per-model detector degradation is demonstrated on exactly those 13 models individually.\\n\"\n181\t  },\n182\t  {\n183\t    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n184\t    \"description\": \"Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items. LLM paraphrasing, original vs reformulated comparison, FDR-controlled detection.\\n\",\n185\t    \"results\": {\n186\t      \"n_items_paraphrased\": 247,\n187\t      \"constat_detection_rate_all\": 0.38,\n188\t      \"constat_detection_rate_sft\": 0.44,\n189\t      \"constat_detection_rate_rl\": 0.12,\n190\t      \"constat_fdr_controlled_rate\": 0.31,\n191\t      \"constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.19,\n192\t      \"constat_enrichment_vs_li\": 1.82,\n193\t      \"constat_fpr_on_clean\": 0.08,\n194\t      \"paraphrase_semantic_similarity\": 0.93,\n195\t      \"paraphrase_quality_human_rating\": 4.2\n196\t    },\n197\t    \"tables\": [\n198\t      {\n199\t        \"name\": \"Table: ConStat Detection Power by Model Type\",\n200\t        \"columns\": [\n201\t          \"Model type\",\n202\t          \"N_models\",\n203\t          \"Detection rate\",\n204\t          \"FDR-corrected\"\n205\t        ],\n206\t        \"rows\": [\n207\t          [\n208\t            \"SFT-only\",\n209\t            50,\n210\t            \"0.44\",\n211\t            \"0.37\"\n212\t          ],\n213\t          [\n214\t            \"Alignment-RL\",\n215\t            50,\n216\t            \"0.31\",\n217\t            \"0.24\"\n218\t          ],\n219\t          [\n220\t            \"Reasoning-RL\",\n221\t            13,\n222\t            \"0.12\",\n223\t            \"0.07\"\n224\t          ],\n225\t          [\n226\t            \"All\",\n227\t            113,\n228\t            \"0.38\",\n229\t            \"0.31\"\n230\t          ]\n231\t        ]\n232\t      }\n233\t    ],\n234\t    \"figures\": [],\n235\t    \"analysis\": \"ConStat results serve the paper as a baseline comparison method. Its strengths (higher Li et al. enrichment) and weaknesses (RL degradation, narrow coverage) are both narratively useful. In the best tier, this experiment is reported in a compressed comparison table rather than as a standalone section, reinforcing Claim 2's meta-critique without bloating the page budget. Moved to appendix in the 8-page version.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, ConStat performs respectably on non-RL models (detection rate 0.44) but degrades sharply on RL models (0.12), confirming the complementary detection hypothesis. The key finding for the paper narrative is the low Jaccard overlap (0.19) between ConStat and DIF flags — these methods identify fundamentally different item properties. ConStat's higher enrichment against Li et al. labels (1.82 vs DIF's 0.96) actually supports the meta-critique: ConStat detects items with web exposure that also show perturbation sensitivity (a subset of globally contaminated items), while DIF captures temporal differential functioning regardless of web exposure. This directly illustrates why web-overlap ground truth works for ConStat-type detectors but not for DIF-type detectors. Reviewers see this as strong empirical support for the claim that different detectors measure different constructs.\\n\"\n236\t  },\n237\t  {\n238\t    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n239\t    \"description\": \"Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU. 10-15 open-weight models, 50 responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection.\\n\",\n240\t    \"results\": {\n241\t      \"n_models_evaluated\": 12,\n242\t      \"n_items_scored\": 12508,\n243\t      \"dvd_detection_rate_overall\": 0.22,\n244\t      \"dvd_detection_rate_variant_contamination\": 0.58,\n245\t      \"dvd_detection_rate_verbatim_contamination\": 0.09,\n246\t      \"dvd_bimodal_pct\": 14.3,\n247\t      \"dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.11,\n248\t      \"dvd_enrichment_vs_li\": 1.41,\n249\t      \"generation_variance_mean_clean\": 0.34,\n250\t      \"generation_variance_mean_contaminated\": 0.18,\n251\t      \"temperature_sensitivity\": \"T=0.7 optimal, T=1.0 noisy\"\n252\t    },\n253\t    \"tables\": [\n254\t      {\n255\t        \"name\": \"Table: DVD Detection Power by Contamination Type\",\n256\t        \"columns\": [\n257\t          \"Contamination type\",\n258\t          \"Detection rate\",\n259\t          \"Generation variance (mean)\"\n260\t        ],\n261\t        \"rows\": [\n262\t          [\n263\t            \"Verbatim\",\n264\t            \"0.09\",\n265\t            \"0.07\"\n266\t          ],\n267\t          [\n268\t            \"Variant (paraphrased)\",\n269\t            \"0.58\",\n270\t            \"0.41\"\n271\t          ],\n272\t          [\n273\t            \"Clean (no contamination)\",\n274\t            \"0.04\",\n275\t            \"0.34\"\n276\t          ]\n277\t        ]\n278\t      }\n279\t    ],\n280\t    \"figures\": [],\n281\t    \"analysis\": \"DVD complements both DIF and ConStat by targeting a contamination mechanism neither captures well. In the best tier, this is reported as supplementary evidence for the fragmentation thesis. Like plan_003a, compressed to appendix in the 8-page version. The key takeaway for reviewers is that three independent detectors with Jaccard<0.20 pairwise overlap proves the field needs method-specific validation, not one-size-fits-all ground truth.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, DVD shows a distinctive detection profile: strong on variant contamination (items memorized in paraphrased form, high variance across generations) but weak on verbatim contamination (where models produce near-deterministic correct answers with low variance). This creates a natural complementarity with DIF, which detects differential functioning regardless of contamination mechanism. The very low Jaccard overlap (0.11) between DVD and DIF flags again reinforces the meta-critique: each detector operates on a different signal, so no single ground truth can validate all approaches. The 14.3% bimodal distribution finding suggests a meaningful fraction of items have split response profiles across model generations — consistent with DIF's temporal instability finding but measuring a different facet. Reviewers accept this as evidence that the contamination detection space is fragmented, motivating the unified validation framework (Claim 3).\\n\"\n282\t  },\n283\t  {\n284\t    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n285\t    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Calibrated thresholds, majority voting, weighted fusion, cross-validated weights.\\n\",\n286\t    \"results\": {\n287\t      \"ensemble_2of3_coverage\": 0.71,\n288\t      \"ensemble_weighted_coverage\": 0.76,\n289\t      \"ensemble_fpr_2of3\": 0.062,\n290\t      \"ensemble_fpr_weighted\": 0.058,\n291\t      \"ensemble_auroc\": 0.89,\n292\t      \"dif_solo_auroc\": 0.91,\n293\t      \"constat_solo_auroc\": 0.72,\n294\t      \"dvd_solo_auroc\": 0.64,\n295\t      \"cv_weight_dif\": 0.52,\n296\t      \"cv_weight_constat\": 0.31,\n297\t      \"cv_weight_dvd\": 0.17,\n298\t      \"uniform_vs_optimized_auroc_diff\": 0.02,\n299\t      \"coverage_by_regime\": {\n300\t        \"large_effect_dif_dominant\": \"δ≥1.4 → DIF TPR=0.76, ensemble=0.78\",\n301\t        \"medium_effect_constat_adds\": \"0.6≤δ<1.4 → DIF TPR=0.31, ensemble=0.54\",\n302\t        \"variant_dvd_adds\": \"variant only → DIF TPR=0.08, ensemble=0.42\"\n303\t      }\n304\t    },\n305\t    \"tables\": [\n306\t      {\n307\t        \"name\": \"Table: Ensemble vs Individual Detector Performance\",\n308\t        \"columns\": [\n309\t          \"Method\",\n310\t          \"AUROC\",\n311\t          \"Coverage@FPR<0.10\",\n312\t          \"Regime strength\"\n313\t        ],\n314\t        \"rows\": [\n315\t          [\n316\t            \"DIF (MH)\",\n317\t            \"0.91\",\n318\t            \"0.68\",\n319\t            \"Large-effect differential\"\n320\t          ],\n321\t          [\n322\t            \"ConStat\",\n323\t            \"0.72\",\n324\t            \"0.44\",\n325\t            \"Medium-effect, non-RL\"\n326\t          ],\n327\t          [\n328\t            \"DVD\",\n329\t            \"0.64\",\n330\t            \"0.31\",\n331\t            \"Variant contamination\"\n332\t          ],\n333\t          [\n334\t            \"Ensemble (2-of-3)\",\n335\t            \"0.89\",\n336\t            \"0.71\",\n337\t            \"Broad coverage\"\n338\t          ],\n339\t          [\n340\t            \"Ensemble (weighted)\",\n341\t            \"0.89\",\n342\t            \"0.76\",\n343\t            \"Broad coverage\"\n344\t          ]\n345\t        ]\n346\t      }\n347\t    ],\n348\t    \"figures\": [],\n349\t    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble fusion demonstrates the complementarity thesis empirically but is not load-bearing for any of the 4 claims. In the best tier, it appears in the appendix as \\\"future direction validated in preliminary form.\\\" The main paper mentions it in one sentence in Discussion: \\\"preliminary ensemble fusion (Appendix X) suggests complementary coverage across detection regimes.\\\" This keeps the paper focused on characterization rather than detection.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the ensemble shows that DIF alone captures most of the large-effect detection power (AUROC 0.91 vs ensemble 0.89 — ensemble slightly lower due to false-positive leakage from weaker signals), but the ensemble provides superior coverage in the medium-effect and variant-contamination regimes where DIF's power drops below the operational threshold. The cross-validated weights heavily favor DIF (0.52) reflecting its stronger signal-to-noise ratio, with ConStat contributing in the SFT regime and DVD in the variant regime. The key finding is that uniform-weight baseline performs within 0.02 AUROC of optimized weights — the ensemble is robust to weight miscalibration. FPR<0.10 across all configurations satisfies the precision constraint. However, in the best tier this is NOT in the main paper — it's appendix material. The narrative has pivoted from \\\"DIF detects contamination\\\" to \\\"DIF characterizes benchmarks,\\\" making ensemble detection a secondary contribution. Reviewers appreciate the thoroughness but don't require it for acceptance.\\n\"\n350\t  },\n351\t  {\n352\t    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n353\t    \"description\": \"Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (METABENCH, 1,319 items × 6,702 models) with GSM1K contamination gap as external validation. Three steps: temporal DIF, semi-synthetic validation, and GSM1K correlation.\\n\",\n354\t    \"results\": {\n355\t      \"step1_gsm8k_temporal_dif\": {\n356\t        \"n_items\": 1319,\n357\t        \"n_ref\": 1017,\n358\t        \"n_foc\": 804,\n359\t        \"standard_c_pct\": 18.9,\n360\t        \"external_c_pct\": 47.4,\n361\t        \"sanity_c_pct\": 0.3,\n362\t        \"standard_external_spearman\": 0.926\n363\t      },\n364\t      \"step2_semi_synthetic\": {\n365\t        \"standard_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n366\t        \"standard_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n367\t        \"external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.517,\n368\t        \"fpr_stable\": true\n369\t      },\n370\t      \"step3_gsm1k_correlation\": {\n371\t        \"n_models\": 33,\n372\t        \"pearson_r\": -0.478,\n373\t        \"pearson_p\": 0.005,\n374\t        \"pearson_bca_ci\": \"[-0.714, -0.155]\",\n375\t        \"spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n376\t        \"spearman_p\": 0.02,\n377\t        \"spearman_bca_ci\": \"[-0.687, -0.031]\",\n378\t        \"partial_r_controlling_accuracy\": -0.385,\n379\t        \"partial_p\": 0.03,\n380\t        \"partial_bca_ci\": \"[-0.640, -0.058]\",\n381\t        \"permutation_p\": 0.014,\n382\t        \"leave_one_out_r_range\": \"[-0.537, -0.374]\",\n383\t        \"cook_d_max_model\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n384\t        \"robust\": true\n385\t      }\n386\t    },\n387\t    \"tables\": [\n388\t      {\n389\t        \"name\": \"Table: GSM1K Cross-Benchmark Validation\",\n390\t        \"columns\": [\n391\t          \"Statistic\",\n392\t          \"Estimate\",\n393\t          \"p-value\",\n394\t          \"95% BCa CI\"\n395\t        ],\n396\t        \"rows\": [\n397\t          [\n398\t            \"Pearson r\",\n399\t            \"-0.478\",\n400\t            \"0.005\",\n401\t            \"[-0.714, -0.155]\"\n402\t          ],\n403\t          [\n404\t            \"Spearman ρ\",\n405\t            \"-0.403\",\n406\t            \"0.020\",\n407\t            \"[-0.687, -0.031]\"\n408\t          ],\n409\t          [\n410\t            \"Partial r (ctrl acc)\",\n411\t            \"-0.385\",\n412\t            \"0.030\",\n413\t            \"[-0.640, -0.058]\"\n414\t          ],\n415\t          [\n416\t            \"Permutation p\",\n417\t            \"—\",\n418\t            \"0.014\",\n419\t            \"—\"\n420\t          ]\n421\t        ]\n422\t      }\n423\t    ],\n424\t    \"figures\": [],\n425\t    \"analysis\": \"GSM8K replication is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence in the paper. In the best tier, the Step 3 correlation survives scrutiny because: (1) all four statistical tests (Pearson, Spearman, partial, permutation) converge on significance, (2) effect direction is theoretically predicted (DIF items resist contamination-based gains), (3) leave-one-out shows robustness. The honest reporting of wide CIs and N=33 limitation actually builds reviewer trust — overclaiming would trigger more skepticism than underclaiming.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the GSM8K replication serves three purposes. Step 1: GSM8K's lower C% (18.9% vs MMLU's 31.3%) is expected — GSM8K is a single-domain math benchmark with less construct heterogeneity, so fewer items exhibit differential functioning. This is actually GOOD — it shows DIF rates are domain-appropriate, not inflated by methodology. Step 2: ΔTPR=+0.818 (standard matching) on GSM8K exceeds MMLU's +0.674, confirming the semi-synthetic framework is portable and more powerful on single-domain benchmarks where matching is cleaner. Step 3: The anti-correlation r=-0.478 (p=0.005) between DIF flags and GSM1K contamination gap is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence. Models with larger contamination gaps (GSM8K >> GSM1K) show disproportionately LOWER performance on DIF-C items — consistent with contamination producing surface gains on non-DIF items while DIF items capture genuine capability. All CIs exclude zero; leave-one-out stability (r range [-0.537, -0.374]) and Cook's D analysis confirm no single influential model drives the result. In the best tier, reviewers accept N=33 as suggestive corroboration (not a standalone proof) because all statistical tests converge and the effect direction is theoretically coherent. The wide CI upper bounds (Pearson |r|=0.155, Spearman |ρ|=0.031) are honestly reported in limitations, and reviewers appreciate the transparency rather than attacking it.\\n\"\n426\t  },\n427\t  {\n428\t    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n429\t    \"description\": \"Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU. Fit bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific factors), select anchor items, fixed-parameter calibration of replacements, demonstrate equated score equivalence.\\n\",\n430\t    \"results\": {\n431\t      \"bifactor_rmsea\": 0.028,\n432\t      \"bifactor_cfi\": 0.91,\n433\t      \"bifactor_tli\": 0.89,\n434\t      \"bifactor_srmr\": 0.042,\n435\t      \"general_factor_variance_explained\": 0.34,\n436\t      \"subject_factor_variance_explained\": 0.41,\n437\t      \"n_anchor_items_selected\": 4200,\n438\t      \"anchor_dif_free_pct\": 100,\n439\t      \"equated_rho_original_vs_replaced\": 0.964,\n440\t      \"equated_tau_original_vs_replaced\": 0.951,\n441\t      \"calibration_convergence_rate\": 0.92,\n442\t      \"mean_equating_error_theta\": 0.08,\n443\t      \"max_equating_error_theta\": 0.31\n444\t    },\n445\t    \"tables\": [\n446\t      {\n447\t        \"name\": \"Table: Bifactor IRT Fit Indices\",\n448\t        \"columns\": [\n449\t          \"Index\",\n450\t          \"Value\",\n451\t          \"Threshold\",\n452\t          \"Verdict\"\n453\t        ],\n454\t        \"rows\": [\n455\t          [\n456\t            \"RMSEA\",\n457\t            \"0.028\",\n458\t            \"<0.05\",\n459\t            \"Good\"\n460\t          ],\n461\t          [\n462\t            \"CFI\",\n463\t            \"0.91\",\n464\t            \">0.95\",\n465\t            \"Marginal\"\n466\t          ],\n467\t          [\n468\t            \"TLI\",\n469\t            \"0.89\",\n470\t            \">0.95\",\n471\t            \"Marginal\"\n472\t          ],\n473\t          [\n474\t            \"SRMR\",\n475\t            \"0.042\",\n476\t            \"<0.08\",\n477\t            \"Good\"\n478\t          ]\n479\t        ]\n480\t      }\n481\t    ],\n482\t    \"figures\": [],\n483\t    \"analysis\": \"Bifactor results serve as proof-of-concept for the equating pipeline, not as a polished contribution. In the best tier, the marginal CFI/TLI values are a feature, not a bug — they empirically demonstrate that MMLU requires multidimensional treatment, supporting the limitations section's call for future MIRT work. The successful equating despite imperfect fit shows the approach is promising enough to warrant dedicated follow-up.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the bifactor model achieves acceptable (not excellent) fit — RMSEA=0.028 is good, but CFI=0.91 and TLI=0.89 are at the conventional threshold, reflecting MMLU's inherent 57-domain multidimensionality that no simple factor structure fully captures. The general factor explains only 34% of variance, with subject-specific factors at 41% — consistent with MMLU being more of a test battery than a unidimensional measure. This is itself an important finding: it empirically quantifies why unidimensional IRT is misspecified for MMLU (explaining the |Lz|>2 misfit observed in 98% of models). The equated rankings achieve ρ=0.964 (above the ≥0.95 target), demonstrating that psychometric equating is feasible even with imperfect model fit. In the best tier, this pilot is reported in 2-3 sentences in Discussion as \\\"bifactor equating is feasible; full development is future work\\\" rather than as a standalone claim. The marginal fit metrics are honestly reported and positioned as motivation for multidimensional IRT extensions.\\n\"\n484\t  },\n485\t  {\n486\t    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n487\t    \"description\": \"Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation, validate difficulty correlation.\\n\",\n488\t    \"results\": {\n489\t      \"n_items_generated\": 500,\n490\t      \"n_dif_flagged_targeted\": 200,\n491\t      \"generation_quality_human_rating\": 3.8,\n492\t      \"difficulty_corr_prompt_based_rho\": 0.32,\n493\t      \"difficulty_corr_student_sim_rho\": 0.71,\n494\t      \"difficulty_corr_empirical_rho\": 0.68,\n495\t      \"construct_alignment_pct\": 0.84,\n496\t      \"cognitive_level_match_pct\": 0.78,\n497\t      \"holdout_panel_rho\": 0.65,\n498\t      \"generation_reject_rate\": 0.22,\n499\t      \"avg_generation_attempts\": 1.8\n500\t    },\n501\t    \"tables\": [\n502\t      {\n503\t        \"name\": \"Table: Item Difficulty Prediction Accuracy\",\n504\t        \"columns\": [\n505\t          \"Method\",\n506\t          \"ρ with empirical difficulty\",\n507\t          \"95% CI\"\n508\t        ],\n509\t        \"rows\": [\n510\t          [\n511\t            \"Prompt-based targeting\",\n512\t            \"0.32\",\n513\t            \"[0.19, 0.44]\"\n514\t          ],\n515\t          [\n516\t            \"Student simulation\",\n517\t            \"0.71\",\n518\t            \"[0.64, 0.77]\"\n519\t          ],\n520\t          [\n521\t            \"Empirical (holdout panel)\",\n522\t            \"0.68\",\n523\t            \"[0.60, 0.75]\"\n524\t          ]\n525\t        ]\n526\t      }\n527\t    ],\n528\t    \"figures\": [],\n529\t    \"analysis\": \"Item generation is explicitly positioned as future work in the best tier. The ρ=0.71 result is promising enough to motivate follow-up but not strong enough to claim as a contribution. In the EMNLP submission, this is at most an appendix entry demonstrating the practical viability of the DIF-informed benchmark maintenance pipeline.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, LLM-generated replacement items achieve reasonable but imperfect quality. The key finding is the gap between prompt-based difficulty targeting (ρ=0.32, consistent with literature's 0.10-0.45) and student simulation calibration (ρ=0.71) — simulation doubles the difficulty prediction accuracy, validating the calibration approach. The empirical correlation (ρ=0.68) slightly lags simulation prediction, reflecting real-world noise that simulation doesn't fully capture. Construct alignment (84%) and cognitive level matching (78%) are adequate for a pilot but below production quality, honestly reported. In the best tier, this appears as 1-2 sentences in Future Work: \\\"preliminary item generation with student simulation calibration achieves ρ=0.71 difficulty correlation, suggesting automated benchmark maintenance is feasible.\\\" Reviewers don't evaluate this as a standalone contribution — it's a forward-looking demonstration.\\n\"\n530\t  },\n531\t  {\n532\t    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n533\t    \"description\": \"Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) over 4 successive benchmark administrations. Show power accumulation for medium effects.\\n\",\n534\t    \"results\": {\n535\t      \"admin_1_power_d10\": 0.38,\n536\t      \"admin_2_power_d10\": 0.54,\n537\t      \"admin_3_power_d10\": 0.69,\n538\t      \"admin_4_power_d10\": 0.78,\n539\t      \"admin_1_power_d14\": 0.68,\n540\t      \"admin_2_power_d14\": 0.82,\n541\t      \"admin_3_power_d14\": 0.91,\n542\t      \"admin_4_power_d14\": 0.95,\n543\t      \"cumulative_fpr_admin4\": 0.048,\n544\t      \"bayes_factor_threshold\": 3.0,\n545\t      \"prior_update_mechanism\": \"posterior from admin t → prior for admin t+1\",\n546\t      \"simulation_n_items\": 1000,\n547\t      \"simulation_n_models_per_admin\": 200\n548\t    },\n549\t    \"tables\": [\n550\t      {\n551\t        \"name\": \"Table: Cumulative Power by Administration Wave\",\n552\t        \"columns\": [\n553\t          \"Administration\",\n554\t          \"Power (δ=1.0)\",\n555\t          \"Power (δ=1.4)\",\n556\t          \"FPR\"\n557\t        ],\n558\t        \"rows\": [\n559\t          [\n560\t            \"1\",\n561\t            \"0.38\",\n562\t            \"0.68\",\n563\t            \"0.042\"\n564\t          ],\n565\t          [\n566\t            \"2\",\n567\t            \"0.54\",\n568\t            \"0.82\",\n569\t            \"0.044\"\n570\t          ],\n571\t          [\n572\t            \"3\",\n573\t            \"0.69\",\n574\t            \"0.91\",\n575\t            \"0.046\"\n576\t          ],\n577\t          [\n578\t            \"4\",\n579\t            \"0.78\",\n580\t            \"0.95\",\n581\t            \"0.048\"\n582\t          ]\n583\t        ]\n584\t      }\n585\t    ],\n586\t    \"figures\": [],\n587\t    \"analysis\": \"Cumulative Bayesian DIF is the most forward-looking experiment in the plan. In the best tier, it provides the theoretical foundation for the Conclusion's recommendation that \\\"benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits periodically\\\" — by showing that cumulative monitoring outperforms repeated one-shot testing. Entirely simulation-based and relegated to appendix, but it gives the paper a constructive vision beyond diagnosis.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the cumulative Bayesian approach shows clean power accumulation: from 0.38 (single administration, δ=1.0) to 0.69 after 3 administrations, crossing the 0.70 power threshold. At δ=1.4 (the operational threshold identified in power simulations), power reaches 0.91 by administration 3 and 0.95 by administration 4. The FPR remains controlled at 0.048 (below 0.05) even after 4 accumulation rounds, confirming the Bayesian framework properly regularizes false positives. This is a simulation-only result — no real multi-wave longitudinal data exists for LLM benchmarks. In the best tier, this is purely appendix material: \\\"Simulation shows cumulative Bayesian DIF achieves 0.69 power at δ=1.0 after 3 administrations, suggesting longitudinal benchmark monitoring is feasible.\\\" Reviewers see it as nice-to-have future work, not a current contribution.\\n\"\n588\t  },\n589\t  {\n590\t    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n591\t    \"description\": \"Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement. Simulate 5-50% replacement rates, measure ranking stability, reliability, coverage.\\n\",\n592\t    \"results\": {\n593\t      \"replacement_5pct_rho\": 0.999,\n594\t      \"replacement_10pct_rho\": 0.998,\n595\t      \"replacement_20pct_rho\": 0.994,\n596\t      \"replacement_50pct_rho\": 0.976,\n597\t      \"reliability_shr_original\": 0.993,\n598\t      \"reliability_shr_5pct\": 0.992,\n599\t      \"reliability_shr_20pct\": 0.987,\n600\t      \"reliability_shr_50pct\": 0.969,\n601\t      \"construct_coverage_original\": 1.0,\n602\t      \"construct_coverage_20pct\": 0.96,\n603\t      \"construct_coverage_50pct\": 0.88,\n604\t      \"theoretical_bound_rho_lower\": \"ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (r = replacement fraction)\",\n605\t      \"critical_replacement_threshold\": 0.35\n606\t    },\n607\t    \"tables\": [\n608\t      {\n609\t        \"name\": \"Table: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement\",\n610\t        \"columns\": [\n611\t          \"Replacement %\",\n612\t          \"ρ (ranking)\",\n613\t          \"SHR (reliability)\",\n614\t          \"Coverage\"\n615\t        ],\n616\t        \"rows\": [\n617\t          [\n618\t            \"0% (baseline)\",\n619\t            \"1.000\",\n620\t            \"0.993\",\n621\t            \"1.00\"\n622\t          ],\n623\t          [\n624\t            \"5%\",\n625\t            \"0.999\",\n626\t            \"0.992\",\n627\t            \"0.99\"\n628\t          ],\n629\t          [\n630\t            \"10%\",\n631\t            \"0.998\",\n632\t            \"0.990\",\n633\t            \"0.98\"\n634\t          ],\n635\t          [\n636\t            \"20%\",\n637\t            \"0.994\",\n638\t            \"0.987\",\n639\t            \"0.96\"\n640\t          ],\n641\t          [\n642\t            \"31% (all DIF)\",\n643\t            \"0.989\",\n644\t            \"0.982\",\n645\t            \"0.93\"\n646\t          ],\n647\t          [\n648\t            \"50%\",\n649\t            \"0.976\",\n650\t            \"0.969\",\n651\t            \"0.88\"\n652\t          ]\n653\t        ]\n654\t      }\n655\t    ],\n656\t    \"figures\": [],\n657\t    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation complements plan_017's ranking stability finding by extending it to the replacement scenario. In the best tier, this empirically grounds the practical recommendation in the Conclusion. The 35% critical threshold being near the 31% DIF rate creates a natural narrative: \\\"the current level of instability is at the edge of MMLU's redundancy buffer — one more model generation may exceed it.\\\" This urgency framing, backed by quantitative bounds, strengthens the paper's call to action without being alarmist.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, graceful degradation shows that MMLU's massive redundancy (already revealed by plan_017's ρ≈0.997 after 31% DIF removal) translates into a wide safe zone for item replacement. Up to 20% replacement, ranking stability stays above 0.99 and reliability above 0.98. The critical threshold at 35% marks where construct coverage drops below 0.95 — interestingly close to the 31% DIF rate, suggesting that replacing all DIF items simultaneously would be at the edge of the safe zone. This is a useful calibration result: benchmark maintainers can replace DIF items in waves (10-15% per refresh) without compromising measurement properties. The theoretical bound ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (where r = replacement fraction) provides a practical planning tool. In the best tier, this appears as 2-3 sentences in Discussion supporting the recommendation that DIF-informed benchmark refresh is operationally safe.\\n\"\n658\t  },\n659\t  {\n660\t    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n661\t    \"description\": \"Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to categorical cohort DIF. Model release date as continuous covariate, compare with MH-DIF.\\n\",\n662\t    \"results\": {\n663\t      \"n_items_analyzed\": 12508,\n664\t      \"ift_significant_temporal_pct\": 34.7,\n665\t      \"ift_vs_mh_c_jaccard\": 0.72,\n666\t      \"ift_mh_concordance_rate\": 0.81,\n667\t      \"ift_additional_detections\": 423,\n668\t      \"ift_missed_by_mh\": 0,\n669\t      \"ift_optimal_split_date\": \"2023-09-15\",\n670\t      \"ift_nonlinear_items_pct\": 8.3,\n671\t      \"ift_computation_time_hours\": 2.4,\n672\t      \"ift_vs_mh_rank_corr_delta_mh\": 0.88\n673\t    },\n674\t    \"tables\": [\n675\t      {\n676\t        \"name\": \"Table: IFT vs MH-DIF Concordance\",\n677\t        \"columns\": [\n678\t          \"Category\",\n679\t          \"MH-C items\",\n680\t          \"IFT-significant items\",\n681\t          \"Overlap\"\n682\t        ],\n683\t        \"rows\": [\n684\t          [\n685\t            \"Both flagged\",\n686\t            \"—\",\n687\t            \"—\",\n688\t            3495\n689\t          ],\n690\t          [\n691\t            \"MH-C only\",\n692\t            \"—\",\n693\t            \"—\",\n694\t            0\n695\t          ],\n696\t          [\n697\t            \"IFT only\",\n698\t            \"—\",\n699\t            \"—\",\n700\t            423\n701\t          ],\n702\t          [\n703\t            \"Neither\",\n704\t            \"—\",\n705\t            \"—\",\n706\t            8590\n707\t          ],\n708\t          [\n709\t            \"Concordance\",\n710\t            \"—\",\n711\t            \"—\",\n712\t            \"0.81\"\n713\t          ]\n714\t        ]\n715\t      }\n716\t    ],\n717\t    \"figures\": [],\n718\t    \"analysis\": \"IFT provides methodological depth and confirms the MH analysis is appropriate. In the best tier, the zero-miss result (MH-C ⊂ IFT) is particularly valuable — it means MH's simpler approach doesn't miss important signals, just captures fewer borderline cases. This defuses any reviewer concern about arbitrary cohort splitting. Relegated to appendix as robustness check.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, Item-Focused Trees confirm the MH-DIF findings while adding nuance. The 34.7% IFT-significant rate slightly exceeds MH's 31.3% because IFT captures gradual temporal trends that MH's binary split misses, plus 8.3% of items show nonlinear temporal patterns (e.g., stability→instability→restabilization). The high concordance (Jaccard=0.72, concordance=0.81) validates MH's simpler approach for the primary analysis, while IFT's additional detections (423 items) represent borderline cases near MH's threshold. The optimal split date (2023-09-15) aligns with the ChatGPT-era capability jump, providing temporal specificity that MH's cohort-level analysis cannot. Zero missed detections (IFT ⊃ MH-C) confirms MH is conservative, supporting the \\\"31% is a lower bound\\\" framing. In the best tier, IFT appears as a robustness check in appendix: \\\"continuous-covariate IFT analysis confirms the temporal DIF pattern (concordance=0.81) and identifies the optimal split near 2023-09, consistent with known capability transitions.\\\"\\n\"\n719\t  },\n720\t  {\n721\t    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n722\t    \"description\": \"Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations. Subset to decoder-only models, compare IRT fit and DIF detection vs full population.\\n\",\n723\t    \"results\": {\n724\t      \"n_decoder_only\": 5226,\n725\t      \"n_non_decoder\": 1,\n726\t      \"decoder_only_pct\": 99.98,\n727\t      \"c_pct_decoder_only\": 31.3,\n728\t      \"c_pct_full\": 31.3,\n729\t      \"jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full\": 1.0,\n730\t      \"delta_mh_rank_corr\": 0.9999,\n731\t      \"irt_rmsea_full\": 0.089,\n732\t      \"irt_rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.088,\n733\t      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_full\": 98.1,\n734\t      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_decoder_only\": 98.0,\n735\t      \"removed_model\": \"RWKV-based (1 model)\"\n736\t    },\n737\t    \"tables\": [\n738\t      {\n739\t        \"name\": \"Table: Architecture Stratification Impact\",\n740\t        \"columns\": [\n741\t          \"Population\",\n742\t          \"N\",\n743\t          \"C%\",\n744\t          \"Jaccard vs full\",\n745\t          \"RMSEA\"\n746\t        ],\n747\t        \"rows\": [\n748\t          [\n749\t            \"Full (all architectures)\",\n750\t            5227,\n751\t            \"31.3\",\n752\t            \"—\",\n753\t            \"0.089\"\n754\t          ],\n755\t          [\n756\t            \"Decoder-only\",\n757\t            5226,\n758\t            \"31.3\",\n759\t            \"1.0\",\n760\t            \"0.088\"\n761\t          ]\n762\t        ]\n763\t      }\n764\t    ],\n765\t    \"figures\": [],\n766\t    \"analysis\": \"Architecture stratification is the simplest experiment in the plan but one of the most valuable rhetorically. In the best tier, the Jaccard=1.0 result is a one-line knockout of a potential reviewer concern. It also reveals an important meta-insight: the METABENCH MMLU ecosystem is architecturally homogeneous, so any observed DIF must reflect temporal/training differences, not architectural ones. This eliminates an entire class of confounds.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the architecture stratification result is maximally clean: removing the single non-decoder-only model (RWKV) changes absolutely nothing (Jaccard=1.0, identical C%). This is because METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only — architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue by construction. The IRT fit metrics barely change because the |Lz|>2 misfit reflects MMLU's 57-domain multidimensionality, not architecture mixing. This result preempts the \\\"spurious DIF from architecture heterogeneity\\\" concern in a single sentence: \\\"METABENCH MMLU contains 5,226/5,227 decoder-only models; excluding the 1 non-decoder model yields identical DIF flags (Jaccard=1.0).\\\" In the best tier, reviewers see this preemptive control as a sign of thoroughness. The persistent |Lz|>2 is discussed in limitations as inherent to applying 1D IRT to a multidimensional test battery — not a methodological failure but a known constraint motivating future MIRT work.\\n\"\n767\t  },\n768\t  {\n769\t    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n770\t    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into real METABENCH response matrix. Controlled ground truth for DIF detection power. Vary injection prevalence, exploitation rate, focal group size.\\n\",\n771\t    \"results\": {\n772\t      \"temporal_cohort_results\": {\n773\t        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.314,\n774\t        \"tpr_p00\": 0.326,\n775\t        \"tpr_p03\": 0.548,\n776\t        \"tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n777\t        \"tpr_p07\": 0.99,\n778\t        \"tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n779\t        \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n780\t        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n781\t        \"fpr_inflation_at_p10\": 0.302\n782\t      },\n783\t      \"ability_cohort_results\": {\n784\t        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.493,\n785\t        \"tpr_p00\": 0.498,\n786\t        \"tpr_p10\": 0.8,\n787\t        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.302\n788\t      },\n789\t      \"external_matching_results\": {\n790\t        \"fpr\": 0.314,\n791\t        \"tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n792\t        \"tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n793\t        \"fpr_stable_across_injection\": true\n794\t      },\n795\t      \"cross_subject_matching_results\": {\n796\t        \"delta_fpr_all_injection_levels\": 0.0,\n797\t        \"tpr_improvement_over_within\": \"4/5 subjects improved\"\n798\t      },\n799\t      \"dose_response_clean\": true,\n800\t      \"exploitation_rate_sensitivity\": {\n801\t        \"p04_tpr\": 0.62,\n802\t        \"p06_tpr\": 0.91,\n803\t        \"p08_tpr\": 0.98\n804\t      }\n805\t    },\n806\t    \"tables\": [\n807\t      {\n808\t        \"name\": \"Table: Semi-Synthetic Validation — Dose-Response\",\n809\t        \"columns\": [\n810\t          \"Exploitation rate (p)\",\n811\t          \"TPR (temporal)\",\n812\t          \"ΔTPR\",\n813\t          \"FPR (external match)\"\n814\t        ],\n815\t        \"rows\": [\n816\t          [\n817\t            \"0.0 (baseline)\",\n818\t            \"0.326\",\n819\t            \"—\",\n820\t            \"0.314\"\n821\t          ],\n822\t          [\n823\t            \"0.3\",\n824\t            \"0.548\",\n825\t            \"+0.222\",\n826\t            \"0.314\"\n827\t          ],\n828\t          [\n829\t            \"0.5\",\n830\t            \"0.834\",\n831\t            \"+0.508\",\n832\t            \"0.314\"\n833\t          ],\n834\t          [\n835\t            \"0.7\",\n836\t            \"0.990\",\n837\t            \"+0.664\",\n838\t            \"0.314\"\n839\t          ],\n840\t          [\n841\t            \"1.0\",\n842\t            \"1.000\",\n843\t            \"+0.674\",\n844\t            \"0.314\"\n845\t          ]\n846\t        ]\n847\t      },\n848\t      {\n849\t        \"name\": \"Table: Matching Strategy Comparison\",\n850\t        \"columns\": [\n851\t          \"Matching strategy\",\n852\t          \"ΔFPR (p=1.0)\",\n853\t          \"ΔTPR (p=0.5)\",\n854\t          \"Verdict\"\n855\t        ],\n856\t        \"rows\": [\n857\t          [\n858\t            \"Standard (within-subject)\",\n859\t            \"+0.302\",\n860\t            \"+0.508\",\n861\t            \"FPR inflated\"\n862\t          ],\n863\t          [\n864\t            \"External (cross-subject)\",\n865\t            \"0.000\",\n866\t            \"+0.508\",\n867\t            \"FPR controlled ✓\"\n868\t          ],\n869\t          [\n870\t            \"Cross-benchmark (GSM8K)\",\n871\t            \"0.000\",\n872\t            \"+0.818\",\n873\t            \"FPR controlled ✓\"\n874\t          ]\n875\t        ]\n876\t      }\n877\t    ],\n878\t    \"figures\": [],\n879\t    \"analysis\": \"The semi-synthetic framework is the paper's methodological contribution to the field — Claim 3 rests entirely on this experiment. In the best tier, the ΔTPR metric is recognized as a principled way to evaluate any item-level contamination detector under controlled conditions, and the cross-subject matching insight is appreciated as a practical recommendation with general applicability. The dose-response linearity gives practitioners intuitive guidance: \\\"at 50% exploitation, DIF detects with ΔTPR≈0.5.\\\" The FPR=0.314 baseline (reflecting the 31.3% temporal DIF rate) is not a false positive problem — it's the true temporal instability rate, which the injected contamination adds to. Reviewers in the best tier understand this distinction.\\n\\nIn the best tier, the semi-synthetic validation provides the controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. The ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates substantial detection power: injecting 50% exploitation rate into 200 items produces a +0.508 increase in true positive rate above the temporal baseline. The dose-response curve is clean and monotonic — from p=0.3 (ΔTPR≈0.22) through p=0.5 (+0.508) to p=1.0 (+0.674) — confirming that DIF detection power scales predictably with contamination intensity. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all injection levels) is the methodological contribution: it eliminates the FPR inflation that plagues within-subject matching (which inflates FPR by +0.186 because the matching variable itself becomes contaminated). This finding has practical value — any future DIF application to multi-domain benchmarks should use cross-subject matching. The ability cohort shows weaker ΔTPR (+0.302) because the baseline FPR is already high (0.493), leaving less room for improvement. In the best tier, the bug fix (inf-delta classified as extreme DIF not class A) is transparently reported as a methodological note, increasing reviewer trust. The matching variable contamination finding is recognized as a subtle and important methodological insight.\\n\"\n880\t  }\n881\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c61f7179-56df-476f-b574-625c8ff520d1","uuid":"5056c143-8323-4dea-ae73-2774c0df3119","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:15:55.844Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n    \"description\": \"Apply MH-DIF to full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models × 12,508 items). Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, architecture stratification, within-family confound control (Llama-2 vs Llama-3), domain-specificity analysis, and proxy validation against Li et al. web-overlap labels.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"temporal_enrichment_vs_li\": 0.96,\n      \"temporal_fpr_on_li_clean\": 0.318,\n      \"ability_quintile_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n      \"ability_quintile_enrichment\": 1.19,\n      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n      \"temporal_ability_jaccard\": 0.014,\n      \"n_subjects_with_dif\": 57,\n      \"subject_c_pct_range\": \"20.7–48.4\",\n      \"subject_c_pct_unweighted_mean\": 30.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 1: DIF Detection Summary by Cohort Definition\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Cohort\",\n          \"N_ref\",\n          \"N_foc\",\n          \"ETS_A%\",\n          \"ETS_B%\",\n          \"ETS_C%\",\n          \"Enrichment\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Temporal (2023 vs 2024-H1)\",\n            1253,\n            656,\n            \"47.8\",\n            \"20.9\",\n            \"31.3\",\n            0.96\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ability (Q1 vs Q5)\",\n            1044,\n            1044,\n            \"89.2\",\n            \"7.9\",\n            \"2.9\",\n            1.19\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random split (sanity)\",\n            960,\n            960,\n            \"97.5\",\n            \"2.3\",\n            \"0.18\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: Within-Family DIF Confound Control\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Family pair\",\n          \"N_ref\",\n          \"N_foc\",\n          \"C%\",\n          \"Interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Llama-2 vs Llama-3\",\n            161,\n            308,\n            \"61.3\",\n            \"Capability gain + architecture shift\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mistral vs Mixtral\",\n            134,\n            89,\n            \"38.7\",\n            \"Architecture shift (dense→MoE)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 31.3% figure anchors the entire paper. Its robustness is demonstrated through three internal controls: (1) ability-only cohort produces 2.9% — proving temporal signal is not an ability artifact, (2) random split produces 0.18% — proving it is not statistical noise, (3) enrichment ≈ 1.0 — proving DIF flags are orthogonal to web-overlap contamination labels. The within-family results are higher than temporal (61.3% for Llama-2v3) because these compare families with known architecture changes, validating DIF sensitivity to genuine cross-generation shifts. In the best tier, reviewers see this not as \\\"DIF fails\\\" but as \\\"DIF correctly identifies that MMLU items function differently across model generations.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the temporal cohort analysis cleanly reveals 31.3% ETS-C items — a number that is striking enough to command attention but not so extreme as to suggest methodological artifact. The near-unity enrichment (0.96) against Li et al. labels is the keystone of the meta-critique: DIF flags are orthogonal to web-overlap labels, confirming that these measure fundamentally different constructs (differential vs global exposure). The ability-quintile cohort shows only 2.9% C-items — a critical internal control proving that when groups differ only in ability (not temporally), DIF correctly produces near-zero flags. The random-split sanity check at 0.18% eliminates statistical artifact concerns entirely. Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is high because these families have maximal temporal separation AND known architecture/training changes — this becomes a positive result showing DIF captures genuine cross-generation functional shifts. Domain ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) is modest but significant, appropriately handled by plan_019 decomposition showing domain improvement explains only 2.4pp. All 57 subjects showing some instability (range 20.7–48.4%) demonstrates this is a benchmark-wide phenomenon, not concentrated in a few pathological domains. Reviewers accept this as the definitive large-scale characterization of MMLU temporal stability.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models. SFT-only baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) mechanism control, and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO) focal models. Two-level validation: group-level DIF stability and model-level traditional detector degradation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_sft_baseline\": 312,\n      \"n_alignment_rl\": 477,\n      \"n_reasoning_rl\": 13,\n      \"dif_c_pct_sft_cohort\": 30.8,\n      \"dif_c_pct_alignment_rl_cohort\": 31.1,\n      \"dif_c_pct_mixed_rl_cohort\": 30.5,\n      \"jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl\": 0.87,\n      \"jaccard_sft_vs_mixed_rl\": 0.84,\n      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.72,\n      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.41,\n      \"mink_auroc_sft\": 0.68,\n      \"mink_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.35,\n      \"dif_retention_ratio_vs_constat\": 0.97,\n      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.008,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_dif_auroc\": \"[0.88, 0.94]\",\n      \"delong_p_dif_vs_constat\": 0.003,\n      \"partial_exploitation_boundary_p\": 0.45\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: DIF Stability Across Cohort Compositions\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Cohort composition\",\n          \"N_focal\",\n          \"C%\",\n          \"Jaccard vs SFT baseline\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"SFT-only focal\",\n            312,\n            \"30.8\",\n            \"1.00\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"+ Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO)\",\n            789,\n            \"31.1\",\n            \"0.87\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"+ Reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\",\n            802,\n            \"30.5\",\n            \"0.84\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Per-Model Detector Degradation on Reasoning-RL\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Detector\",\n          \"AUROC (SFT)\",\n          \"AUROC (Reasoning-RL)\",\n          \"Δ\",\n          \"DeLong p\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"DIF (group-level)\",\n            \"0.91\",\n            \"0.90\",\n            \"-0.01\",\n            \"0.82\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ConStat\",\n            \"0.72\",\n            \"0.41\",\n            \"-0.31\",\n            \"0.003\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Min-K%\",\n            \"0.68\",\n            \"0.35\",\n            \"-0.33\",\n            \"0.001\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This experiment serves a dual rhetorical purpose: (1) it shows DIF is robust to the specific threat model (RL concealing contamination signals) that motivates the paper, and (2) it provides a direct head-to-head comparison with existing per-model detectors. In the best tier, the N=13 limitation is acknowledged but reviewers accept the two-level design as sufficient — DIF's group-level nature means the validation logic is sound even with small RL samples. The partial exploitation analysis adds theoretical depth, characterizing when DIF works rather than just showing that it does.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, DIF's group-level nature makes it inherently robust to RL training: MH conditions on total score within the cohort, so individual model training methodology is irrelevant as long as the cohort composition doesn't systematically shift. With only 13 reasoning-RL models added to a focal group of 656+, the cohort composition barely changes (Jaccard 0.84 with baseline). The C% remains within 1pp of baseline across all three cohort compositions, confirming group-level stability. Meanwhile, ConStat and Min-K% — which operate at the individual model level — degrade sharply on reasoning-RL models (AUROC drops from 0.72/0.68 to 0.41/0.35) because GRPO/PPO flatten the NLL landscape these detectors rely on. TOST equivalence testing (p=0.008) formally confirms DIF retention is not just \\\"failed to reject null\\\" but positively equivalent. The partial exploitation analysis shows DIF detection boundary at p≈0.45, meaning contamination must affect <45% of focal group to evade — a reasonable operational bound. Reviewers accept the N=13 reasoning-RL sample because DIF's claim is group-level robustness (adding 13 models doesn't change group statistics), while per-model detector degradation is demonstrated on exactly those 13 models individually.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items. LLM paraphrasing, original vs reformulated comparison, FDR-controlled detection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_paraphrased\": 247,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_all\": 0.38,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_sft\": 0.44,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_rl\": 0.12,\n      \"constat_fdr_controlled_rate\": 0.31,\n      \"constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.19,\n      \"constat_enrichment_vs_li\": 1.82,\n      \"constat_fpr_on_clean\": 0.08,\n      \"paraphrase_semantic_similarity\": 0.93,\n      \"paraphrase_quality_human_rating\": 4.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: ConStat Detection Power by Model Type\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model type\",\n          \"N_models\",\n          \"Detection rate\",\n          \"FDR-corrected\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"SFT-only\",\n            50,\n            \"0.44\",\n            \"0.37\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Alignment-RL\",\n            50,\n            \"0.31\",\n            \"0.24\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Reasoning-RL\",\n            13,\n            \"0.12\",\n            \"0.07\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"All\",\n            113,\n            \"0.38\",\n            \"0.31\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ConStat results serve the paper as a baseline comparison method. Its strengths (higher Li et al. enrichment) and weaknesses (RL degradation, narrow coverage) are both narratively useful. In the best tier, this experiment is reported in a compressed comparison table rather than as a standalone section, reinforcing Claim 2's meta-critique without bloating the page budget. Moved to appendix in the 8-page version.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, ConStat performs respectably on non-RL models (detection rate 0.44) but degrades sharply on RL models (0.12), confirming the complementary detection hypothesis. The key finding for the paper narrative is the low Jaccard overlap (0.19) between ConStat and DIF flags — these methods identify fundamentally different item properties. ConStat's higher enrichment against Li et al. labels (1.82 vs DIF's 0.96) actually supports the meta-critique: ConStat detects items with web exposure that also show perturbation sensitivity (a subset of globally contaminated items), while DIF captures temporal differential functioning regardless of web exposure. This directly illustrates why web-overlap ground truth works for ConStat-type detectors but not for DIF-type detectors. Reviewers see this as strong empirical support for the claim that different detectors measure different constructs.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU. 10-15 open-weight models, 50 responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models_evaluated\": 12,\n      \"n_items_scored\": 12508,\n      \"dvd_detection_rate_overall\": 0.22,\n      \"dvd_detection_rate_variant_contamination\": 0.58,\n      \"dvd_detection_rate_verbatim_contamination\": 0.09,\n      \"dvd_bimodal_pct\": 14.3,\n      \"dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.11,\n      \"dvd_enrichment_vs_li\": 1.41,\n      \"generation_variance_mean_clean\": 0.34,\n      \"generation_variance_mean_contaminated\": 0.18,\n      \"temperature_sensitivity\": \"T=0.7 optimal, T=1.0 noisy\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: DVD Detection Power by Contamination Type\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Contamination type\",\n          \"Detection rate\",\n          \"Generation variance (mean)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Verbatim\",\n            \"0.09\",\n            \"0.07\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Variant (paraphrased)\",\n            \"0.58\",\n            \"0.41\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Clean (no contamination)\",\n            \"0.04\",\n            \"0.34\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"DVD complements both DIF and ConStat by targeting a contamination mechanism neither captures well. In the best tier, this is reported as supplementary evidence for the fragmentation thesis. Like plan_003a, compressed to appendix in the 8-page version. The key takeaway for reviewers is that three independent detectors with Jaccard<0.20 pairwise overlap proves the field needs method-specific validation, not one-size-fits-all ground truth.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, DVD shows a distinctive detection profile: strong on variant contamination (items memorized in paraphrased form, high variance across generations) but weak on verbatim contamination (where models produce near-deterministic correct answers with low variance). This creates a natural complementarity with DIF, which detects differential functioning regardless of contamination mechanism. The very low Jaccard overlap (0.11) between DVD and DIF flags again reinforces the meta-critique: each detector operates on a different signal, so no single ground truth can validate all approaches. The 14.3% bimodal distribution finding suggests a meaningful fraction of items have split response profiles across model generations — consistent with DIF's temporal instability finding but measuring a different facet. Reviewers accept this as evidence that the contamination detection space is fragmented, motivating the unified validation framework (Claim 3).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Calibrated thresholds, majority voting, weighted fusion, cross-validated weights.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"ensemble_2of3_coverage\": 0.71,\n      \"ensemble_weighted_coverage\": 0.76,\n      \"ensemble_fpr_2of3\": 0.062,\n      \"ensemble_fpr_weighted\": 0.058,\n      \"ensemble_auroc\": 0.89,\n      \"dif_solo_auroc\": 0.91,\n      \"constat_solo_auroc\": 0.72,\n      \"dvd_solo_auroc\": 0.64,\n      \"cv_weight_dif\": 0.52,\n      \"cv_weight_constat\": 0.31,\n      \"cv_weight_dvd\": 0.17,\n      \"uniform_vs_optimized_auroc_diff\": 0.02,\n      \"coverage_by_regime\": {\n        \"large_effect_dif_dominant\": \"δ≥1.4 → DIF TPR=0.76, ensemble=0.78\",\n        \"medium_effect_constat_adds\": \"0.6≤δ<1.4 → DIF TPR=0.31, ensemble=0.54\",\n        \"variant_dvd_adds\": \"variant only → DIF TPR=0.08, ensemble=0.42\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Ensemble vs Individual Detector Performance\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"AUROC\",\n          \"Coverage@FPR<0.10\",\n          \"Regime strength\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"DIF (MH)\",\n            \"0.91\",\n            \"0.68\",\n            \"Large-effect differential\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ConStat\",\n            \"0.72\",\n            \"0.44\",\n            \"Medium-effect, non-RL\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"DVD\",\n            \"0.64\",\n            \"0.31\",\n            \"Variant contamination\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ensemble (2-of-3)\",\n            \"0.89\",\n            \"0.71\",\n            \"Broad coverage\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ensemble (weighted)\",\n            \"0.89\",\n            \"0.76\",\n            \"Broad coverage\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble fusion demonstrates the complementarity thesis empirically but is not load-bearing for any of the 4 claims. In the best tier, it appears in the appendix as \\\"future direction validated in preliminary form.\\\" The main paper mentions it in one sentence in Discussion: \\\"preliminary ensemble fusion (Appendix X) suggests complementary coverage across detection regimes.\\\" This keeps the paper focused on characterization rather than detection.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the ensemble shows that DIF alone captures most of the large-effect detection power (AUROC 0.91 vs ensemble 0.89 — ensemble slightly lower due to false-positive leakage from weaker signals), but the ensemble provides superior coverage in the medium-effect and variant-contamination regimes where DIF's power drops below the operational threshold. The cross-validated weights heavily favor DIF (0.52) reflecting its stronger signal-to-noise ratio, with ConStat contributing in the SFT regime and DVD in the variant regime. The key finding is that uniform-weight baseline performs within 0.02 AUROC of optimized weights — the ensemble is robust to weight miscalibration. FPR<0.10 across all configurations satisfies the precision constraint. However, in the best tier this is NOT in the main paper — it's appendix material. The narrative has pivoted from \\\"DIF detects contamination\\\" to \\\"DIF characterizes benchmarks,\\\" making ensemble detection a secondary contribution. Reviewers appreciate the thoroughness but don't require it for acceptance.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n    \"description\": \"Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (METABENCH, 1,319 items × 6,702 models) with GSM1K contamination gap as external validation. Three steps: temporal DIF, semi-synthetic validation, and GSM1K correlation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"step1_gsm8k_temporal_dif\": {\n        \"n_items\": 1319,\n        \"n_ref\": 1017,\n        \"n_foc\": 804,\n        \"standard_c_pct\": 18.9,\n        \"external_c_pct\": 47.4,\n        \"sanity_c_pct\": 0.3,\n        \"standard_external_spearman\": 0.926\n      },\n      \"step2_semi_synthetic\": {\n        \"standard_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n        \"standard_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n        \"external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.517,\n        \"fpr_stable\": true\n      },\n      \"step3_gsm1k_correlation\": {\n        \"n_models\": 33,\n        \"pearson_r\": -0.478,\n        \"pearson_p\": 0.005,\n        \"pearson_bca_ci\": \"[-0.714, -0.155]\",\n        \"spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n        \"spearman_p\": 0.02,\n        \"spearman_bca_ci\": \"[-0.687, -0.031]\",\n        \"partial_r_controlling_accuracy\": -0.385,\n        \"partial_p\": 0.03,\n        \"partial_bca_ci\": \"[-0.640, -0.058]\",\n        \"permutation_p\": 0.014,\n        \"leave_one_out_r_range\": \"[-0.537, -0.374]\",\n        \"cook_d_max_model\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n        \"robust\": true\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: GSM1K Cross-Benchmark Validation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Statistic\",\n          \"Estimate\",\n          \"p-value\",\n          \"95% BCa CI\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Pearson r\",\n            \"-0.478\",\n            \"0.005\",\n            \"[-0.714, -0.155]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Spearman ρ\",\n            \"-0.403\",\n            \"0.020\",\n            \"[-0.687, -0.031]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Partial r (ctrl acc)\",\n            \"-0.385\",\n            \"0.030\",\n            \"[-0.640, -0.058]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Permutation p\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.014\",\n            \"—\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"GSM8K replication is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence in the paper. In the best tier, the Step 3 correlation survives scrutiny because: (1) all four statistical tests (Pearson, Spearman, partial, permutation) converge on significance, (2) effect direction is theoretically predicted (DIF items resist contamination-based gains), (3) leave-one-out shows robustness. The honest reporting of wide CIs and N=33 limitation actually builds reviewer trust — overclaiming would trigger more skepticism than underclaiming.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the GSM8K replication serves three purposes. Step 1: GSM8K's lower C% (18.9% vs MMLU's 31.3%) is expected — GSM8K is a single-domain math benchmark with less construct heterogeneity, so fewer items exhibit differential functioning. This is actually GOOD — it shows DIF rates are domain-appropriate, not inflated by methodology. Step 2: ΔTPR=+0.818 (standard matching) on GSM8K exceeds MMLU's +0.674, confirming the semi-synthetic framework is portable and more powerful on single-domain benchmarks where matching is cleaner. Step 3: The anti-correlation r=-0.478 (p=0.005) between DIF flags and GSM1K contamination gap is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence. Models with larger contamination gaps (GSM8K >> GSM1K) show disproportionately LOWER performance on DIF-C items — consistent with contamination producing surface gains on non-DIF items while DIF items capture genuine capability. All CIs exclude zero; leave-one-out stability (r range [-0.537, -0.374]) and Cook's D analysis confirm no single influential model drives the result. In the best tier, reviewers accept N=33 as suggestive corroboration (not a standalone proof) because all statistical tests converge and the effect direction is theoretically coherent. The wide CI upper bounds (Pearson |r|=0.155, Spearman |ρ|=0.031) are honestly reported in limitations, and reviewers appreciate the transparency rather than attacking it.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n    \"description\": \"Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU. Fit bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific factors), select anchor items, fixed-parameter calibration of replacements, demonstrate equated score equivalence.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bifactor_rmsea\": 0.028,\n      \"bifactor_cfi\": 0.91,\n      \"bifactor_tli\": 0.89,\n      \"bifactor_srmr\": 0.042,\n      \"general_factor_variance_explained\": 0.34,\n      \"subject_factor_variance_explained\": 0.41,\n      \"n_anchor_items_selected\": 4200,\n      \"anchor_dif_free_pct\": 100,\n      \"equated_rho_original_vs_replaced\": 0.964,\n      \"equated_tau_original_vs_replaced\": 0.951,\n      \"calibration_convergence_rate\": 0.92,\n      \"mean_equating_error_theta\": 0.08,\n      \"max_equating_error_theta\": 0.31\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Bifactor IRT Fit Indices\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Index\",\n          \"Value\",\n          \"Threshold\",\n          \"Verdict\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"RMSEA\",\n            \"0.028\",\n            \"<0.05\",\n            \"Good\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"CFI\",\n            \"0.91\",\n            \">0.95\",\n            \"Marginal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"TLI\",\n            \"0.89\",\n            \">0.95\",\n            \"Marginal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"SRMR\",\n            \"0.042\",\n            \"<0.08\",\n            \"Good\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Bifactor results serve as proof-of-concept for the equating pipeline, not as a polished contribution. In the best tier, the marginal CFI/TLI values are a feature, not a bug — they empirically demonstrate that MMLU requires multidimensional treatment, supporting the limitations section's call for future MIRT work. The successful equating despite imperfect fit shows the approach is promising enough to warrant dedicated follow-up.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the bifactor model achieves acceptable (not excellent) fit — RMSEA=0.028 is good, but CFI=0.91 and TLI=0.89 are at the conventional threshold, reflecting MMLU's inherent 57-domain multidimensionality that no simple factor structure fully captures. The general factor explains only 34% of variance, with subject-specific factors at 41% — consistent with MMLU being more of a test battery than a unidimensional measure. This is itself an important finding: it empirically quantifies why unidimensional IRT is misspecified for MMLU (explaining the |Lz|>2 misfit observed in 98% of models). The equated rankings achieve ρ=0.964 (above the ≥0.95 target), demonstrating that psychometric equating is feasible even with imperfect model fit. In the best tier, this pilot is reported in 2-3 sentences in Discussion as \\\"bifactor equating is feasible; full development is future work\\\" rather than as a standalone claim. The marginal fit metrics are honestly reported and positioned as motivation for multidimensional IRT extensions.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n    \"description\": \"Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation, validate difficulty correlation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_generated\": 500,\n      \"n_dif_flagged_targeted\": 200,\n      \"generation_quality_human_rating\": 3.8,\n      \"difficulty_corr_prompt_based_rho\": 0.32,\n      \"difficulty_corr_student_sim_rho\": 0.71,\n      \"difficulty_corr_empirical_rho\": 0.68,\n      \"construct_alignment_pct\": 0.84,\n      \"cognitive_level_match_pct\": 0.78,\n      \"holdout_panel_rho\": 0.65,\n      \"generation_reject_rate\": 0.22,\n      \"avg_generation_attempts\": 1.8\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Item Difficulty Prediction Accuracy\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Method\",\n          \"ρ with empirical difficulty\",\n          \"95% CI\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Prompt-based targeting\",\n            \"0.32\",\n            \"[0.19, 0.44]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Student simulation\",\n            \"0.71\",\n            \"[0.64, 0.77]\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Empirical (holdout panel)\",\n            \"0.68\",\n            \"[0.60, 0.75]\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Item generation is explicitly positioned as future work in the best tier. The ρ=0.71 result is promising enough to motivate follow-up but not strong enough to claim as a contribution. In the EMNLP submission, this is at most an appendix entry demonstrating the practical viability of the DIF-informed benchmark maintenance pipeline.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, LLM-generated replacement items achieve reasonable but imperfect quality. The key finding is the gap between prompt-based difficulty targeting (ρ=0.32, consistent with literature's 0.10-0.45) and student simulation calibration (ρ=0.71) — simulation doubles the difficulty prediction accuracy, validating the calibration approach. The empirical correlation (ρ=0.68) slightly lags simulation prediction, reflecting real-world noise that simulation doesn't fully capture. Construct alignment (84%) and cognitive level matching (78%) are adequate for a pilot but below production quality, honestly reported. In the best tier, this appears as 1-2 sentences in Future Work: \\\"preliminary item generation with student simulation calibration achieves ρ=0.71 difficulty correlation, suggesting automated benchmark maintenance is feasible.\\\" Reviewers don't evaluate this as a standalone contribution — it's a forward-looking demonstration.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) over 4 successive benchmark administrations. Show power accumulation for medium effects.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"admin_1_power_d10\": 0.38,\n      \"admin_2_power_d10\": 0.54,\n      \"admin_3_power_d10\": 0.69,\n      \"admin_4_power_d10\": 0.78,\n      \"admin_1_power_d14\": 0.68,\n      \"admin_2_power_d14\": 0.82,\n      \"admin_3_power_d14\": 0.91,\n      \"admin_4_power_d14\": 0.95,\n      \"cumulative_fpr_admin4\": 0.048,\n      \"bayes_factor_threshold\": 3.0,\n      \"prior_update_mechanism\": \"posterior from admin t → prior for admin t+1\",\n      \"simulation_n_items\": 1000,\n      \"simulation_n_models_per_admin\": 200\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Cumulative Power by Administration Wave\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Administration\",\n          \"Power (δ=1.0)\",\n          \"Power (δ=1.4)\",\n          \"FPR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1\",\n            \"0.38\",\n            \"0.68\",\n            \"0.042\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2\",\n            \"0.54\",\n            \"0.82\",\n            \"0.044\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3\",\n            \"0.69\",\n            \"0.91\",\n            \"0.046\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"4\",\n            \"0.78\",\n            \"0.95\",\n            \"0.048\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cumulative Bayesian DIF is the most forward-looking experiment in the plan. In the best tier, it provides the theoretical foundation for the Conclusion's recommendation that \\\"benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits periodically\\\" — by showing that cumulative monitoring outperforms repeated one-shot testing. Entirely simulation-based and relegated to appendix, but it gives the paper a constructive vision beyond diagnosis.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the cumulative Bayesian approach shows clean power accumulation: from 0.38 (single administration, δ=1.0) to 0.69 after 3 administrations, crossing the 0.70 power threshold. At δ=1.4 (the operational threshold identified in power simulations), power reaches 0.91 by administration 3 and 0.95 by administration 4. The FPR remains controlled at 0.048 (below 0.05) even after 4 accumulation rounds, confirming the Bayesian framework properly regularizes false positives. This is a simulation-only result — no real multi-wave longitudinal data exists for LLM benchmarks. In the best tier, this is purely appendix material: \\\"Simulation shows cumulative Bayesian DIF achieves 0.69 power at δ=1.0 after 3 administrations, suggesting longitudinal benchmark monitoring is feasible.\\\" Reviewers see it as nice-to-have future work, not a current contribution.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement. Simulate 5-50% replacement rates, measure ranking stability, reliability, coverage.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"replacement_5pct_rho\": 0.999,\n      \"replacement_10pct_rho\": 0.998,\n      \"replacement_20pct_rho\": 0.994,\n      \"replacement_50pct_rho\": 0.976,\n      \"reliability_shr_original\": 0.993,\n      \"reliability_shr_5pct\": 0.992,\n      \"reliability_shr_20pct\": 0.987,\n      \"reliability_shr_50pct\": 0.969,\n      \"construct_coverage_original\": 1.0,\n      \"construct_coverage_20pct\": 0.96,\n      \"construct_coverage_50pct\": 0.88,\n      \"theoretical_bound_rho_lower\": \"ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (r = replacement fraction)\",\n      \"critical_replacement_threshold\": 0.35\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Replacement %\",\n          \"ρ (ranking)\",\n          \"SHR (reliability)\",\n          \"Coverage\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0% (baseline)\",\n            \"1.000\",\n            \"0.993\",\n            \"1.00\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"5%\",\n            \"0.999\",\n            \"0.992\",\n            \"0.99\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"10%\",\n            \"0.998\",\n            \"0.990\",\n            \"0.98\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"20%\",\n            \"0.994\",\n            \"0.987\",\n            \"0.96\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"31% (all DIF)\",\n            \"0.989\",\n            \"0.982\",\n            \"0.93\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"50%\",\n            \"0.976\",\n            \"0.969\",\n            \"0.88\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation complements plan_017's ranking stability finding by extending it to the replacement scenario. In the best tier, this empirically grounds the practical recommendation in the Conclusion. The 35% critical threshold being near the 31% DIF rate creates a natural narrative: \\\"the current level of instability is at the edge of MMLU's redundancy buffer — one more model generation may exceed it.\\\" This urgency framing, backed by quantitative bounds, strengthens the paper's call to action without being alarmist.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, graceful degradation shows that MMLU's massive redundancy (already revealed by plan_017's ρ≈0.997 after 31% DIF removal) translates into a wide safe zone for item replacement. Up to 20% replacement, ranking stability stays above 0.99 and reliability above 0.98. The critical threshold at 35% marks where construct coverage drops below 0.95 — interestingly close to the 31% DIF rate, suggesting that replacing all DIF items simultaneously would be at the edge of the safe zone. This is a useful calibration result: benchmark maintainers can replace DIF items in waves (10-15% per refresh) without compromising measurement properties. The theoretical bound ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (where r = replacement fraction) provides a practical planning tool. In the best tier, this appears as 2-3 sentences in Discussion supporting the recommendation that DIF-informed benchmark refresh is operationally safe.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n    \"description\": \"Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative to categorical cohort DIF. Model release date as continuous covariate, compare with MH-DIF.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_analyzed\": 12508,\n      \"ift_significant_temporal_pct\": 34.7,\n      \"ift_vs_mh_c_jaccard\": 0.72,\n      \"ift_mh_concordance_rate\": 0.81,\n      \"ift_additional_detections\": 423,\n      \"ift_missed_by_mh\": 0,\n      \"ift_optimal_split_date\": \"2023-09-15\",\n      \"ift_nonlinear_items_pct\": 8.3,\n      \"ift_computation_time_hours\": 2.4,\n      \"ift_vs_mh_rank_corr_delta_mh\": 0.88\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: IFT vs MH-DIF Concordance\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Category\",\n          \"MH-C items\",\n          \"IFT-significant items\",\n          \"Overlap\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Both flagged\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            3495\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MH-C only\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            0\n          ],\n          [\n            \"IFT only\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            423\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Neither\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            8590\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Concordance\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.81\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"IFT provides methodological depth and confirms the MH analysis is appropriate. In the best tier, the zero-miss result (MH-C ⊂ IFT) is particularly valuable — it means MH's simpler approach doesn't miss important signals, just captures fewer borderline cases. This defuses any reviewer concern about arbitrary cohort splitting. Relegated to appendix as robustness check.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, Item-Focused Trees confirm the MH-DIF findings while adding nuance. The 34.7% IFT-significant rate slightly exceeds MH's 31.3% because IFT captures gradual temporal trends that MH's binary split misses, plus 8.3% of items show nonlinear temporal patterns (e.g., stability→instability→restabilization). The high concordance (Jaccard=0.72, concordance=0.81) validates MH's simpler approach for the primary analysis, while IFT's additional detections (423 items) represent borderline cases near MH's threshold. The optimal split date (2023-09-15) aligns with the ChatGPT-era capability jump, providing temporal specificity that MH's cohort-level analysis cannot. Zero missed detections (IFT ⊃ MH-C) confirms MH is conservative, supporting the \\\"31% is a lower bound\\\" framing. In the best tier, IFT appears as a robustness check in appendix: \\\"continuous-covariate IFT analysis confirms the temporal DIF pattern (concordance=0.81) and identifies the optimal split near 2023-09, consistent with known capability transitions.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations. Subset to decoder-only models, compare IRT fit and DIF detection vs full population.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_decoder_only\": 5226,\n      \"n_non_decoder\": 1,\n      \"decoder_only_pct\": 99.98,\n      \"c_pct_decoder_only\": 31.3,\n      \"c_pct_full\": 31.3,\n      \"jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full\": 1.0,\n      \"delta_mh_rank_corr\": 0.9999,\n      \"irt_rmsea_full\": 0.089,\n      \"irt_rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.088,\n      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_full\": 98.1,\n      \"irt_lz_pct_above_2_decoder_only\": 98.0,\n      \"removed_model\": \"RWKV-based (1 model)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Architecture Stratification Impact\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Population\",\n          \"N\",\n          \"C%\",\n          \"Jaccard vs full\",\n          \"RMSEA\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Full (all architectures)\",\n            5227,\n            \"31.3\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.089\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Decoder-only\",\n            5226,\n            \"31.3\",\n            \"1.0\",\n            \"0.088\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Architecture stratification is the simplest experiment in the plan but one of the most valuable rhetorically. In the best tier, the Jaccard=1.0 result is a one-line knockout of a potential reviewer concern. It also reveals an important meta-insight: the METABENCH MMLU ecosystem is architecturally homogeneous, so any observed DIF must reflect temporal/training differences, not architectural ones. This eliminates an entire class of confounds.\\n\\n\\nIn the best tier, the architecture stratification result is maximally clean: removing the single non-decoder-only model (RWKV) changes absolutely nothing (Jaccard=1.0, identical C%). This is because METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only — architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue by construction. The IRT fit metrics barely change because the |Lz|>2 misfit reflects MMLU's 57-domain multidimensionality, not architecture mixing. This result preempts the \\\"spurious DIF from architecture heterogeneity\\\" concern in a single sentence: \\\"METABENCH MMLU contains 5,226/5,227 decoder-only models; excluding the 1 non-decoder model yields identical DIF flags (Jaccard=1.0).\\\" In the best tier, reviewers see this preemptive control as a sign of thoroughness. The persistent |Lz|>2 is discussed in limitations as inherent to applying 1D IRT to a multidimensional test battery — not a methodological failure but a known constraint motivating future MIRT work.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into real METABENCH response matrix. Controlled ground truth for DIF detection power. Vary injection prevalence, exploitation rate, focal group size.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_cohort_results\": {\n        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.314,\n        \"tpr_p00\": 0.326,\n        \"tpr_p03\": 0.548,\n        \"tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n        \"tpr_p07\": 0.99,\n        \"tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n        \"delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n        \"fpr_inflation_at_p10\": 0.302\n      },\n      \"ability_cohort_results\": {\n        \"baseline_fpr\": 0.493,\n        \"tpr_p00\": 0.498,\n        \"tpr_p10\": 0.8,\n        \"delta_tpr_p10\": 0.302\n      },\n      \"external_matching_results\": {\n        \"fpr\": 0.314,\n        \"tpr_p05\": 0.834,\n        \"tpr_p10\": 1.0,\n        \"fpr_stable_across_injection\": true\n      },\n      \"cross_subject_matching_results\": {\n        \"delta_fpr_all_injection_levels\": 0.0,\n        \"tpr_improvement_over_within\": \"4/5 subjects improved\"\n      },\n      \"dose_response_clean\": true,\n      \"exploitation_rate_sensitivity\": {\n        \"p04_tpr\": 0.62,\n        \"p06_tpr\": 0.91,\n        \"p08_tpr\": 0.98\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Semi-Synthetic Validation — Dose-Response\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Exploitation rate (p)\",\n          \"TPR (temporal)\",\n          \"ΔTPR\",\n          \"FPR (external match)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0.0 (baseline)\",\n            \"0.326\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"0.314\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.3\",\n            \"0.548\",\n            \"+0.222\",\n            \"0.314\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.5\",\n            \"0.834\",\n            \"+0.508\",\n            \"0.314\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"0.7\",\n            \"0.990\",\n            \"+0.664\",\n            \"0.314\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1.0\",\n            \"1.000\",\n            \"+0.674\",\n            \"0.314\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Matching Strategy Comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Matching strategy\",\n          \"ΔFPR (p=1.0)\",\n          \"ΔTPR (p=0.5)\",\n          \"Verdict\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Standard (within-subject)\",\n            \"+0.302\",\n            \"+0.508\",\n            \"FPR inflated\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"External (cross-subject)\",\n            \"0.000\",\n            \"+0.508\",\n            \"FPR controlled ✓\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Cross-benchmark (GSM8K)\",\n            \"0.000\",\n            \"+0.818\",\n            \"FPR controlled ✓\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The semi-synthetic framework is the paper's methodological contribution to the field — Claim 3 rests entirely on this experiment. In the best tier, the ΔTPR metric is recognized as a principled way to evaluate any item-level contamination detector under controlled conditions, and the cross-subject matching insight is appreciated as a practical recommendation with general applicability. The dose-response linearity gives practitioners intuitive guidance: \\\"at 50% exploitation, DIF detects with ΔTPR≈0.5.\\\" The FPR=0.314 baseline (reflecting the 31.3% temporal DIF rate) is not a false positive problem — it's the true temporal instability rate, which the injected contamination adds to. Reviewers in the best tier understand this distinction.\\n\\nIn the best tier, the semi-synthetic validation provides the controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. The ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates substantial detection power: injecting 50% exploitation rate into 200 items produces a +0.508 increase in true positive rate above the temporal baseline. The dose-response curve is clean and monotonic — from p=0.3 (ΔTPR≈0.22) through p=0.5 (+0.508) to p=1.0 (+0.674) — confirming that DIF detection power scales predictably with contamination intensity. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all injection levels) is the methodological contribution: it eliminates the FPR inflation that plagues within-subject matching (which inflates FPR by +0.186 because the matching variable itself becomes contaminated). This finding has practical value — any future DIF application to multi-domain benchmarks should use cross-subject matching. The ability cohort shows weaker ΔTPR (+0.302) because the baseline FPR is already high (0.493), leaving less room for improvement. In the best tier, the bug fix (inf-delta classified as extreme DIF not class A) is transparently reported as a methodological note, increasing reviewer trust. The matching variable contamination finding is recognized as a subtle and important methodological insight.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":881,"startLine":1,"totalLines":881}}}
