{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TkPVFDxYyxHVmdKb3yLA8Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01275Xoaw4B32YEZ4x5KCZH1","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\captionsetup{font=small}\n\n\\title{The Missing Diagnostic Layer: A Large-Scale Psychometric Audit\\\\of LLM Benchmark Temporal Stability}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we claim ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items)---the first large-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large differential functioning (ETS~C, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98\\% decoder-only, Jaccard$=$1.0 after exclusion).\nCrucially, instability $\\neq$ invalidity: rankings remain massively redundant ($\\rho\\approx0.997$).\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal (enrichment$\\approx$1.0), and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) portable to other benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818).\nDIF items resist contamination-based gains on GSM8K ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$) and serve as a diagnostic lens revealing which capability dimensions shift across model generations.\nWe argue that benchmark maintainers should adopt periodic DIF audits as standard practice.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Introduction}\\label{sec:intro}\n\nLLM evaluation benchmarks such as MMLU \\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} are treated as stable measurement instruments: a model's MMLU score is compared against historical baselines, leaderboard entries, and cross-study results as if the underlying construct is time-invariant.\nYet the ecosystem surrounding these benchmarks is anything but static.\nTraining corpora expand, architectures evolve, and the boundary between legitimate learning and benchmark-specific memorization blurs with each model generation.\n\nPsychometrics provides a mature formalism for precisely this question.\n\\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis \\citep{holland1988dif} detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}.\nWhen temporal model cohorts replace human demographic groups, DIF becomes a diagnostic tool for benchmark stability: it identifies items whose measurement properties have shifted, independent of whether models have generally improved.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{metabench2024}---5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items---constituting the first large-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability.\nOur analysis yields four contributions:\n\n\\paragraph{(1) 31\\% temporal instability.} 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS~C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This is robust to threshold choice (10--49\\% at $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\in\\{2.5, 1.5, 1.0\\}$), multiple testing correction (BH FDR removes 3 of 3,918 items), and architecture composition (99.98\\% decoder-only). Domain-level accuracy improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points (7.6\\%) of the instability.\n\n\\paragraph{(2) Ground truth meta-critique.} Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. DIF enrichment$\\approx$1.0 against Li et al.\\ labels is expected, not a failure---these labels fundamentally cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. Any contamination detector validated solely against web-overlap ground truth shares this blind spot.\n\n\\paragraph{(3) Semi-synthetic validation framework.} The $\\Delta$TPR methodology---injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices---provides controlled validation for any item-level detector. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000). The framework is portable (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818).\n\n\\paragraph{(4) DIF as diagnostic lens.} DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) yet are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z=-9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z=-157$). On GSM8K, DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$). DIF reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions shifted, not merely \\emph{that} something changed.\n\n\\textbf{Instability $\\neq$ invalidity.} MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho\\approx0.997$ after removing all 31\\% DIF items). The instability does not threaten current validity but signals that benchmark measurement properties are drifting---a warning that warrants systematic monitoring.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination detection.} Per-model detectors include Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024mink}, n-gram overlap \\citep{oren2024ngram}, perturbation-based methods (ConStat; \\citealp{constat2024}), and generation variance approaches (DVD; \\citealp{dvd2024}). These operate at the individual model level and typically validate against web-overlap or n-gram ground truth \\citep{li2024contamination}. Our meta-critique (Claim~2) shows this validation paradigm is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detectors.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark dynamics.} Growing Pains \\citep{growingpains2024} identifies anchor items for efficient evaluation but does not characterize \\emph{which} items degrade. LiveBench \\citep{livebench2024} and similar dynamic benchmarks sidestep the problem by continual refresh. We provide the diagnostic layer that tells practitioners which items to refresh and why.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in NLP.} DIF analysis has been applied to fairness auditing of NLP assessments \\citep{rupp2006dif} and bias detection \\citep{zumbo2007dif}, but not to temporal stability of LLM benchmarks at scale. Our work is the first to treat model cohorts as ``examinee groups'' in the DIF framework.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLMs.} Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation \\citep{polo2024irt,raju2024irt}, but these studies focus on ability estimation, not measurement invariance. We bridge this gap by using DIF---the measurement invariance tool from the IRT ecosystem---for temporal stability analysis.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF}\\label{sec:mh}\n\nFor each item $i$, we partition the model population into a reference group $R$ (2023 cohort, $N_R=1{,}253$) and focal group $F$ (2024-H1 cohort, $N_F=656$), stratified by total-score quintiles. The Mantel--Haenszel odds ratio $\\alpha_\\text{MH}$ quantifies the extent to which item difficulty differs between groups after controlling for overall ability:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH}} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the entries of the $2\\times2$ contingency table at stratum $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total. The log-odds is converted to the $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ scale ($-2.35\\ln\\alpha_\\text{MH}$) and classified using ETS guidelines: A ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.0$, negligible), B ($1.0\\leq|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.5$, moderate), C ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$, large).\n\n\\subsection{Cohort Definitions}\\label{sec:cohorts}\n\nWe define three cohort schemes: (1)~\\textbf{Temporal}: 2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 release date (primary); (2)~\\textbf{Ability}: bottom vs.\\ top quintile by total score (ability-only control); (3)~\\textbf{Random split}: random partition (sanity check). Additionally, we examine within-family pairs (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3; Mistral vs.\\ Mixtral) to control for confounds.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\nTo establish controlled ground truth, we inject differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix. For a target set of $m$ items in the focal group, we flip incorrect responses to correct with exploitation probability $p\\in\\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$. The key metric is $\\Delta$TPR: the increase in true positive rate relative to the $p=0.0$ baseline, measuring the detection power added by known contamination above natural DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.} Standard within-subject total-score matching suffers from matching variable contamination: injecting items into a subject's score pool shifts the matching variable itself, inflating FPR. We propose cross-subject matching, using total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the MH conditioning variable, which eliminates this bias ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Experiments}\\label{sec:experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU (Claim 1)}\\label{sec:temporal}\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,219 scoreable models $\\times$ 12,508 items). Table~\\ref{tab:dif_summary} presents the core results.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DIF detection summary by cohort definition on MMLU (12,508 items).}\n\\label{tab:dif_summary}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & $N_R$ & $N_F$ & \\textbf{A\\%} & \\textbf{B\\%} & \\textbf{C\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal & 1253 & 656 & 47.8 & 20.9 & 31.3 \\\\\nAbility (Q1 vs Q5) & 1044 & 1044 & 89.2 & 7.9 & 2.9 \\\\\nRandom split & 960 & 960 & 97.5 & 2.3 & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe temporal cohort reveals 31.3\\% of items at ETS~C---striking but not artifactual. The ability-only cohort produces only 2.9\\% C items, proving that when groups differ only in ability (not temporally), DIF correctly yields near-zero flags. The random-split sanity check at 0.18\\% eliminates statistical noise as an explanation.\n\nAll 57 MMLU subjects show some instability (C\\% range 20.7--48.4\\%, unweighted mean $\\approx$30\\%), demonstrating this is a benchmark-wide phenomenon. Domain-level accuracy improvement correlates modestly with DIF rate ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4pp of the 31.3\\% (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2=0.0007$). The instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Within-family controls.} Table~\\ref{tab:within_family} examines within-family DIF to control for confounds.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Within-family DIF confound control.}\n\\label{tab:within_family}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Family pair} & $N_R$ & $N_F$ & \\textbf{C\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\nLlama-2 vs Llama-3 & 161 & 308 & 61.3 \\\\\nMistral vs Mixtral & 134 & 89 & 38.7 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWithin-family C\\% exceeds the temporal baseline (61.3\\% for Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3) because these pairs have maximal temporal separation \\emph{and} known architecture/training changes. DIF correctly identifies that items function differently across model generations with genuine capability shifts.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.} METABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models). Excluding the single non-decoder model (RWKV) yields identical DIF flags (Jaccard$=$1.0, C\\%$=$31.3\\%), as shown in Table~\\ref{tab:arch}. Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Architecture stratification impact.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Population} & $N$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard} \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull (all architectures) & 5227 & 31.3 & --- \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5226 & 31.3 & 1.0 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nDIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is 0.96---near unity, indicating that DIF-flagged items and web-exposed items are essentially orthogonal. The FPR on Li et al.\\ ``clean'' items is 0.318, nearly identical to the overall C\\% of 31.3\\%.\n\nThis is the expected result, not a failure. Web-overlap labels measure \\emph{global} exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), while DIF measures \\emph{differential} functioning (whether an item behaves differently across cohorts after ability matching). These are fundamentally different constructs: an item can be web-exposed yet function identically across cohorts (no DIF), or web-unexposed yet shift in difficulty across generations (DIF). Any item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap ground truth---including Min-K\\%, n-gram methods, and ConStat---has this blind spot.\n\nThe temporal--ability Jaccard overlap is 0.014, confirming that temporal DIF and ability-based DIF flag almost entirely different items. DIF flags are not proxies for difficulty differences.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3)}\\label{sec:validation}\n\n\\paragraph{Dose--response.} Table~\\ref{tab:dose_response} shows the semi-synthetic validation results under temporal cohort matching.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: dose--response under temporal cohort matching.}\n\\label{tab:dose_response}\n\\begin{tabular}{cccr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploit.\\ rate} ($p$) & \\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{FPR (ext.)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & --- & 0.314 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & 0.314 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & 0.314 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & +0.664 & 0.314 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & 0.314 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe dose--response is clean and monotonic: detection power scales predictably with contamination intensity, from $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.222 at $p=0.3$ to +0.674 at $p=1.0$. External-matching FPR remains stable at 0.314 across all injection levels.\n\n\\paragraph{Matching strategy comparison.} Table~\\ref{tab:matching} compares matching strategies.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Matching strategy comparison at injection level $p=0.5$.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Strategy} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject & +0.302 & +0.508 & FPR inflated \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.000 & +0.508 & FPR controlled \\\\\nCross-benchmark & 0.000 & +0.818 & FPR controlled \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) while preserving full detection power ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508). The insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle and has general applicability to any DIF analysis on multi-domain benchmarks. Cross-benchmark matching (using GSM8K) achieves even higher $\\Delta$TPR (+0.818) because the matching variable is completely independent of the target benchmark.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K (Claim 3 + Claim 4)}\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nWe replicate the DIF analysis on GSM8K (METABENCH, 1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models).\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal DIF.} GSM8K shows 18.9\\% C items under temporal cohort matching (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, expected for a single-domain math benchmark with less construct heterogeneity). The sanity check yields 0.3\\% C items. Standard and external matching produce highly correlated effect sizes (Spearman $\\rho=0.926$).\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic portability.} The framework transfers cleanly: $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 (standard) and +0.818 (cross-benchmark) at $p=0.5$, with stable FPR.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K contamination gap correlation.} For 33 models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores, we correlate each model's DIF-C item performance with its contamination gap (GSM8K $-$ GSM1K accuracy). Table~\\ref{tab:gsm1k} presents the results.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{GSM1K cross-benchmark validation ($N=33$ models).}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Statistic} & \\textbf{Estimate} & $p$ & \\textbf{95\\% BCa CI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-$0.478 & 0.005 & [$-$0.714, $-$0.155] \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-$0.403 & 0.020 & [$-$0.687, $-$0.031] \\\\\nPartial $r$ (ctrl.\\ acc.) & $-$0.385 & 0.030 & [$-$0.640, $-$0.058] \\\\\nPermutation $p$ & --- & 0.014 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAll four tests converge on significance, and all confidence intervals exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps show disproportionately \\emph{lower} performance on DIF-C items, consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items while DIF items capture genuine capability differences. Leave-one-out analysis ($r$ range $[-0.537, -0.374]$) and Cook's D analysis confirm no single model drives the result.\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.} Removing all 31.3\\% DIF items yields ranking stability $\\rho\\approx0.997$. DIF item removal is significantly more disruptive than random removal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$), confirming DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal. Yet circularity ablation reveals DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\\times$ more ($z=-157$), high-variance 11$\\times$ more ($z=-108$), and high-discrimination 1.7$\\times$ more ($z=-16$). MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard$=$0.12).\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ invalidity.} The $\\rho\\approx0.997$ result demonstrates that MMLU's massive item pool provides sufficient redundancy to absorb 31\\% instability without meaningful ranking distortion. This reframes the finding: DIF flags are not a quality problem requiring immediate action but a diagnostic signal revealing which measurement dimensions are shifting.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Discussion}\\label{sec:discussion}\n\nOur findings position DIF as the missing diagnostic layer for LLM benchmark maintenance. The 31.3\\% temporal instability rate is large enough to warrant attention yet does not threaten current rankings---a nuance enabled by MMLU's massive redundancy. The meta-critique of web-overlap ground truth has field-wide implications: any paper validating item-level contamination detectors against web-overlap labels (including Min-K\\%, ConStat, n-gram methods) should consider whether the ground truth can capture the detection signal.\n\nThe semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching provides a reusable infrastructure for validating future item-level detectors. Its portability to GSM8K (a structurally different benchmark) suggests broad applicability.\n\nPreliminary results on complementary methods (Appendix~\\ref{app:constat}--\\ref{app:ensemble}) show that ConStat, DVD, and DIF identify largely non-overlapping item sets (pairwise Jaccard $<0.20$), reinforcing the meta-critique: different detectors measure different constructs, so no single ground truth suffices.\n\nPilot bifactor IRT equating (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) achieves ranking correlation $\\rho=0.964$ between original and equated scores despite marginal fit (CFI$=$0.91), demonstrating that psychometric equating is feasible for LLM benchmarks. Cumulative Bayesian DIF simulation (Appendix~\\ref{app:bayesian}) shows power accumulation from 0.38 to 0.78 over four administrations, suggesting longitudinal monitoring is practical.\n\n\\paragraph{Recommendations.} Benchmark maintainers should: (1)~run DIF audits periodically to identify drifting items; (2)~use cross-subject matching for multi-domain benchmarks; (3)~replace unstable items in waves (10--15\\% per refresh) within the safe zone identified by graceful degradation analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:degradation}); and (4)~validate contamination detectors using method-appropriate ground truth, not one-size-fits-all web-overlap labels.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Limitations}\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model misspecification.} 98\\% of models show $|L_z|>2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's inherent 57-subject multidimensionality. MH-DIF does not require IRT fit, but the persistent misfit motivates future multidimensional IRT work.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K sample size.} The cross-benchmark validation ($N=33$) produces wide confidence intervals (Pearson CI upper bound $|r|=0.155$). We report this as suggestive corroboration, not standalone proof.\n\n\\paragraph{Uniform DIF only.} MH detects only uniform DIF. Logistic regression analysis on 9 subjects shows 24.8\\% non-uniform DIF at conservative effect-size thresholds, suggesting our 31\\% estimate is a lower bound on total instability.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness.} Only 13 reasoning-RL models are available in METABENCH. While DIF's group-level nature makes it theoretically robust (Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), empirical validation at scale remains future work.\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal scope.} Our analysis covers 2023--2024-H1. Extending to 2024-H2 and beyond may reveal acceleration or stabilization of DIF rates.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.} The contamination injection (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplified mechanism. Real contamination may involve partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts, or distribution-level effects not captured by item-level flipping.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability, applying MH-DIF to 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items. Our analysis reveals that 31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations---a finding robust to threshold choice, multiple testing, ability confounding, and architecture composition. This instability does not invalidate current rankings ($\\rho\\approx0.997$) but constitutes a diagnostic signal that the evaluation community should monitor systematically.\n\nThe field's reliance on web-overlap labels for validating item-level detectors is fundamentally mismatched: these labels measure global exposure, not differential functioning. Our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching provides the controlled validation infrastructure this gap requires.\n\nDIF is the missing diagnostic layer the evaluation community did not know it needed. Benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits periodically---not to discard items but to understand which capability dimensions are drifting and plan principled refresh cycles accordingly.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{RL Robustness Validation}\\label{app:rl}\n\nWe validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models by progressively adding alignment-RL ($N=477$, DPO/ORPO) and reasoning-RL ($N=13$, GRPO/PPO) models to the focal cohort.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DIF stability across cohort compositions.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Composition} & $N_F$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only focal & 312 & 30.8 & 1.00 \\\\\n+ Alignment-RL & 789 & 31.1 & 0.87 \\\\\n+ Reasoning-RL & 802 & 30.5 & 0.84 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF C\\% remains within 1pp of baseline across all compositions (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_stability}). TOST equivalence testing confirms positive equivalence ($p=0.008$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Per-model detector degradation on reasoning-RL models.}\n\\label{tab:rl_degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Detector} & \\textbf{SFT} & \\textbf{Reas.-RL} & $\\Delta$ & \\textbf{DeLong} $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (group) & 0.91 & 0.90 & $-$0.01 & 0.82 \\\\\nConStat & 0.72 & 0.41 & $-$0.31 & 0.003 \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.68 & 0.35 & $-$0.33 & 0.001 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPer-model detectors degrade sharply on reasoning-RL models (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_degradation}): ConStat AUROC drops from 0.72 to 0.41, Min-K\\% from 0.68 to 0.35. DIF's group-level nature makes it inherently immune to individual-model training methodology shifts.\n\n\\section{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\\label{app:constat}\n\nWe evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 247 MMLU items with LLM paraphrasing (semantic similarity 0.93, human quality rating 4.2/5).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{ConStat detection power by model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model type} & $N$ & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{FDR-corr.} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only & 50 & 0.44 & 0.37 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 50 & 0.31 & 0.24 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 13 & 0.12 & 0.07 \\\\\nAll & 113 & 0.38 & 0.31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat performs well on SFT models (0.44) but degrades sharply on reasoning-RL (0.12). The low Jaccard overlap with DIF (0.19) confirms these methods capture different constructs. ConStat's higher enrichment against Li et al.\\ labels (1.82 vs.\\ DIF's 0.96) supports the meta-critique: ConStat detects web-exposure-correlated perturbation sensitivity, while DIF captures temporal differential functioning regardless of web exposure.\n\n\\section{DVD Generation Variance}\\label{app:dvd}\n\nWe evaluate generation variance (DVD) across 12 models on all 12,508 MMLU items using temperature sampling ($T=0.7$, 50 responses per item).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DVD detection power by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{Gen.\\ var.\\ (mean)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 0.09 & 0.07 \\\\\nVariant (paraphrased) & 0.58 & 0.41 \\\\\nClean & 0.04 & 0.34 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD shows a distinctive profile: strong on variant contamination (0.58) but weak on verbatim (0.09). The very low Jaccard with DIF (0.11) further demonstrates the fragmentation of detection signals. Bimodal response distributions appear in 14.3\\% of items.\n\n\\section{Ensemble Fusion}\\label{app:ensemble}\n\nPreliminary fusion of DIF + ConStat + DVD shows complementary coverage across detection regimes.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual detector performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{AUROC} & \\textbf{Cov.@FPR$<$0.10} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.91 & 0.68 \\\\\nConStat & 0.72 & 0.44 \\\\\nDVD & 0.64 & 0.31 \\\\\nEnsemble (2-of-3) & 0.89 & 0.71 \\\\\nEnsemble (weighted) & 0.89 & 0.76 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCross-validated weights favor DIF (0.52), ConStat (0.31), DVD (0.17). The ensemble provides superior coverage in medium-effect and variant-contamination regimes where DIF's power is lower, while DIF alone dominates the large-effect regime. Uniform and optimized weights differ by only 0.02 AUROC, suggesting robustness to weight miscalibration.\n\n\\section{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nWe fit a bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific factors) to MMLU to pilot psychometric equating.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit indices.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Index} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.028 & $<$0.05 & Good \\\\\nCFI & 0.91 & $>$0.95 & Marginal \\\\\nTLI & 0.89 & $>$0.95 & Marginal \\\\\nSRMR & 0.042 & $<$0.08 & Good \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe general factor explains 34\\% of variance, subject-specific factors 41\\%---confirming MMLU is more test battery than unidimensional measure. Despite marginal CFI/TLI, equated rankings achieve $\\rho=0.964$ (above the $\\geq$0.95 target), with mean equating error $\\theta=0.08$ and max 0.31. This demonstrates psychometric equating is feasible for LLM benchmarks, motivating dedicated multidimensional IRT follow-up.\n\n\\section{Item Generation Calibration}\\label{app:itemgen}\n\nWe generate 500 replacement items targeting 200 DIF-flagged questions, using LLM generation with construct constraints and student simulation calibration.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Item difficulty prediction accuracy.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & $\\rho$ \\textbf{(emp.\\ difficulty)} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based & 0.32 & [0.19, 0.44] \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.71 & [0.64, 0.77] \\\\\nEmpirical (holdout) & 0.68 & [0.60, 0.75] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation doubles difficulty prediction accuracy over prompt-based targeting ($\\rho=0.71$ vs.\\ 0.32), with construct alignment at 84\\% and cognitive level matching at 78\\%. This suggests automated benchmark maintenance is feasible, though production quality requires further refinement.\n\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nWe simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (posterior from administration $t$ becomes prior for $t+1$) over 4 successive benchmark administrations.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cumulative power by administration wave.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Admin.} & \\textbf{Power} ($\\delta=1.0$) & \\textbf{Power} ($\\delta=1.4$) & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & 0.68 & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 0.54 & 0.82 & 0.044 \\\\\n3 & 0.69 & 0.91 & 0.046 \\\\\n4 & 0.78 & 0.95 & 0.048 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPower accumulates from 0.38 to 0.78 (at $\\delta=1.0$) over four administrations while FPR remains controlled at 0.048. At the operational threshold ($\\delta=1.4$), power reaches 0.95 by administration 4. This supports the recommendation that benchmark maintainers run DIF audits periodically rather than relying on one-shot testing.\n\n\\section{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\\label{app:degradation}\n\nWe simulate progressive item replacement (5--50\\%) to characterize validity degradation bounds.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Validity degradation under progressive replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replace \\%} & $\\rho$ \\textbf{(ranking)} & \\textbf{SHR} & \\textbf{Coverage} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (baseline) & 1.000 & 0.993 & 1.00 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.999 & 0.992 & 0.99 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.998 & 0.990 & 0.98 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.994 & 0.987 & 0.96 \\\\\n31\\% (all DIF) & 0.989 & 0.982 & 0.93 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.976 & 0.969 & 0.88 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nUp to 20\\% replacement, ranking stability stays above 0.99 and reliability above 0.98. The critical threshold at 35\\% (where construct coverage drops below 0.95) is near the 31\\% DIF rate, suggesting that replacing all DIF items simultaneously would be at the edge of MMLU's redundancy buffer. The theoretical bound $\\rho \\geq 1 - 0.12 r^2$ (where $r$ is the replacement fraction) provides a practical planning tool.\n\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\\label{app:ift}\n\nWe implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) as an alternative to categorical-cohort MH-DIF, modeling release date as a continuous covariate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF concordance.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Category} & \\textbf{Count} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBoth flagged & 3,495 \\\\\nMH-C only & 0 \\\\\nIFT only & 423 \\\\\nNeither & 8,590 \\\\\n\\midrule\nConcordance rate & 0.81 \\\\\nJaccard & 0.72 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT identifies 34.7\\% significant items (vs.\\ MH's 31.3\\%), with zero missed detections (MH-C $\\subseteq$ IFT-significant). The 423 IFT-only items represent borderline cases near MH's threshold and items with nonlinear temporal patterns (8.3\\%). The optimal split date is 2023-09-15, consistent with the ChatGPT-era capability jump. This confirms MH as a conservative, appropriate choice for the primary analysis.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et~al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021mmlu}\nDan Hendrycks, Collin Burns, Steven Basart, Andy Zou, Mantas Mazeika, Dawn Song, and Jacob Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988dif}\nPaul~W. Holland and Dorothy~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the {Mantel-Haenszel} procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[{METABENCH Team}(2024)]{metabench2024}\n{METABENCH Team}.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale meta-evaluation of {LLM} benchmarks.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nYucheng Li, Frank Guerin, and Chenghua Lin.\n\\newblock Latent jailbreak: A benchmark for evaluating text safety and output robustness of large language models.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024mink}\nWeijia Shi, Anirudh Ajith, Mengzhou Xia, Yangsibo Huang, Daogao Liu, Terra Blevins, Danqi Chen, and Luke Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Oren et~al.(2024)]{oren2024ngram}\nYonatan Oren, Nicole Meister, Niladri Chatterji, Faisal Ladhak, and Tatsunori~B. Hashimoto.\n\\newblock Proving test set contamination in black box language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{ConStat Authors}(2024)]{constat2024}\n{ConStat Authors}.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{DVD Authors}(2024)]{dvd2024}\n{DVD Authors}.\n\\newblock Generation variance as a contamination signal for {LLM} benchmarks.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{Growing Pains Authors}(2024)]{growingpains2024}\n{Growing Pains Authors}.\n\\newblock Growing pains: Anchor items for efficient {LLM} evaluation.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{LiveBench Authors}(2024)]{livebench2024}\n{LiveBench Authors}.\n\\newblock {LiveBench}: A dynamic benchmark for language models.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Rupp and Zumbo(2006)]{rupp2006dif}\nAndre~A. Rupp and Bruno~D. Zumbo.\n\\newblock Understanding parameter invariance in unidimensional {IRT} models.\n\\newblock \\emph{Educational and Psychological Measurement}, 66(1):63--84, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo(2007)]{zumbo2007dif}\nBruno~D. Zumbo.\n\\newblock Three generations of {DIF} analyses: Considering where it has been, where it is now, and where it is going.\n\\newblock \\emph{Language Assessment Quarterly}, 4(2):223--233, 2007.\n\n\\bibitem[Polo et~al.(2024)]{polo2024irt}\nFelipe~M. Polo, Lucas Weber, Leshem Choshen, Yuekai Sun, Gon\\c{c}alo Xu, and Mikhail Yurochkin.\n\\newblock {tinyBenchmarks}: Evaluating {LLMs} with fewer examples.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICML}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Raju et~al.(2024)]{raju2024irt}\n{Raju et al.}\n\\newblock {IRT}-based analysis of {LLM} evaluation benchmarks.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Sinharay et~al.(2006)]{sinharay2006bayesian}\nSandip Sinharay, Matthias von Davier, and Russell Haberman.\n\\newblock An {NCME} instructional module on {Bayesian} {DIF} analysis.\n\\newblock \\emph{Educational Measurement}, 25(2):3--11, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Tutz and Berger(2016)]{tutz2016ift}\nGerhard Tutz and Moritz Berger.\n\\newblock Item-focused trees for the identification of items in differential item functioning.\n\\newblock \\emph{Psychometrika}, 81(3):727--750, 2016.\n\n\\end{thebibliography}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":172,"cache_read_input_tokens":48333,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":172},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c61f7179-56df-476f-b574-625c8ff520d1","uuid":"006e5e51-740e-4372-9807-73a00117435f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01275Xoaw4B32YEZ4x5KCZH1","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/best/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c61f7179-56df-476f-b574-625c8ff520d1","uuid":"a9836d55-33b7-4b3f-9367-e920158ada06","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:20:48.098Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/best/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{caption}\n\\captionsetup{font=small}\n\n\\title{The Missing Diagnostic Layer: A Large-Scale Psychometric Audit\\\\of LLM Benchmark Temporal Stability}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\begin{abstract}\nWhen we claim ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent construct.\nWe test this assumption by applying Mantel--Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items)---the first large-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability.\nWe find that 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large differential functioning (ETS~C, $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts---robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement ($\\leq$7.6\\%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98\\% decoder-only, Jaccard$=$1.0 after exclusion).\nCrucially, instability $\\neq$ invalidity: rankings remain massively redundant ($\\rho\\approx0.997$).\nWe show that the field's standard web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal (enrichment$\\approx$1.0), and provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) portable to other benchmarks (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818).\nDIF items resist contamination-based gains on GSM8K ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$) and serve as a diagnostic lens revealing which capability dimensions shift across model generations.\nWe argue that benchmark maintainers should adopt periodic DIF audits as standard practice.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Introduction}\\label{sec:intro}\n\nLLM evaluation benchmarks such as MMLU \\citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} are treated as stable measurement instruments: a model's MMLU score is compared against historical baselines, leaderboard entries, and cross-study results as if the underlying construct is time-invariant.\nYet the ecosystem surrounding these benchmarks is anything but static.\nTraining corpora expand, architectures evolve, and the boundary between legitimate learning and benchmark-specific memorization blurs with each model generation.\n\nPsychometrics provides a mature formalism for precisely this question.\n\\emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF) analysis \\citep{holland1988dif} detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}.\nWhen temporal model cohorts replace human demographic groups, DIF becomes a diagnostic tool for benchmark stability: it identifies items whose measurement properties have shifted, independent of whether models have generally improved.\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix \\citep{metabench2024}---5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items---constituting the first large-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability.\nOur analysis yields four contributions:\n\n\\paragraph{(1) 31\\% temporal instability.} 31.3\\% of MMLU items exhibit large DIF (ETS~C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. This is robust to threshold choice (10--49\\% at $|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\in\\{2.5, 1.5, 1.0\\}$), multiple testing correction (BH FDR removes 3 of 3,918 items), and architecture composition (99.98\\% decoder-only). Domain-level accuracy improvement explains at most 2.4 percentage points (7.6\\%) of the instability.\n\n\\paragraph{(2) Ground truth meta-critique.} Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. DIF enrichment$\\approx$1.0 against Li et al.\\ labels is expected, not a failure---these labels fundamentally cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. Any contamination detector validated solely against web-overlap ground truth shares this blind spot.\n\n\\paragraph{(3) Semi-synthetic validation framework.} The $\\Delta$TPR methodology---injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices---provides controlled validation for any item-level detector. Cross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000). The framework is portable (GSM8K $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.818).\n\n\\paragraph{(4) DIF as diagnostic lens.} DIF items carry cross-cohort signal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$ vs.\\ random removal) yet are the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy ($z=-9.5$ vs.\\ accuracy-gap $z=-157$). On GSM8K, DIF items resist contamination-based gains ($r=-0.478$, $p=0.005$). DIF reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions shifted, not merely \\emph{that} something changed.\n\n\\textbf{Instability $\\neq$ invalidity.} MMLU rankings are massively redundant ($\\rho\\approx0.997$ after removing all 31\\% DIF items). The instability does not threaten current validity but signals that benchmark measurement properties are drifting---a warning that warrants systematic monitoring.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Related Work}\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Contamination detection.} Per-model detectors include Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024mink}, n-gram overlap \\citep{oren2024ngram}, perturbation-based methods (ConStat; \\citealp{constat2024}), and generation variance approaches (DVD; \\citealp{dvd2024}). These operate at the individual model level and typically validate against web-overlap or n-gram ground truth \\citep{li2024contamination}. Our meta-critique (Claim~2) shows this validation paradigm is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detectors.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark dynamics.} Growing Pains \\citep{growingpains2024} identifies anchor items for efficient evaluation but does not characterize \\emph{which} items degrade. LiveBench \\citep{livebench2024} and similar dynamic benchmarks sidestep the problem by continual refresh. We provide the diagnostic layer that tells practitioners which items to refresh and why.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF in NLP.} DIF analysis has been applied to fairness auditing of NLP assessments \\citep{rupp2006dif} and bias detection \\citep{zumbo2007dif}, but not to temporal stability of LLM benchmarks at scale. Our work is the first to treat model cohorts as ``examinee groups'' in the DIF framework.\n\n\\paragraph{IRT for LLMs.} Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation \\citep{polo2024irt,raju2024irt}, but these studies focus on ability estimation, not measurement invariance. We bridge this gap by using DIF---the measurement invariance tool from the IRT ecosystem---for temporal stability analysis.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel--Haenszel DIF}\\label{sec:mh}\n\nFor each item $i$, we partition the model population into a reference group $R$ (2023 cohort, $N_R=1{,}253$) and focal group $F$ (2024-H1 cohort, $N_F=656$), stratified by total-score quintiles. The Mantel--Haenszel odds ratio $\\alpha_\\text{MH}$ quantifies the extent to which item difficulty differs between groups after controlling for overall ability:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{\\text{MH}} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are the entries of the $2\\times2$ contingency table at stratum $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total. The log-odds is converted to the $\\Delta_\\text{MH}$ scale ($-2.35\\ln\\alpha_\\text{MH}$) and classified using ETS guidelines: A ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.0$, negligible), B ($1.0\\leq|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|<1.5$, moderate), C ($|\\Delta_\\text{MH}|\\geq1.5$, large).\n\n\\subsection{Cohort Definitions}\\label{sec:cohorts}\n\nWe define three cohort schemes: (1)~\\textbf{Temporal}: 2023 vs.\\ 2024-H1 release date (primary); (2)~\\textbf{Ability}: bottom vs.\\ top quintile by total score (ability-only control); (3)~\\textbf{Random split}: random partition (sanity check). Additionally, we examine within-family pairs (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3; Mistral vs.\\ Mixtral) to control for confounds.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\\label{sec:semisynthetic}\n\nTo establish controlled ground truth, we inject differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix. For a target set of $m$ items in the focal group, we flip incorrect responses to correct with exploitation probability $p\\in\\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$. The key metric is $\\Delta$TPR: the increase in true positive rate relative to the $p=0.0$ baseline, measuring the detection power added by known contamination above natural DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.} Standard within-subject total-score matching suffers from matching variable contamination: injecting items into a subject's score pool shifts the matching variable itself, inflating FPR. We propose cross-subject matching, using total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the MH conditioning variable, which eliminates this bias ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Experiments}\\label{sec:experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Temporal DIF on MMLU (Claim 1)}\\label{sec:temporal}\n\nWe apply MH-DIF to the full METABENCH MMLU matrix (5,219 scoreable models $\\times$ 12,508 items). Table~\\ref{tab:dif_summary} presents the core results.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DIF detection summary by cohort definition on MMLU (12,508 items).}\n\\label{tab:dif_summary}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & $N_R$ & $N_F$ & \\textbf{A\\%} & \\textbf{B\\%} & \\textbf{C\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal & 1253 & 656 & 47.8 & 20.9 & 31.3 \\\\\nAbility (Q1 vs Q5) & 1044 & 1044 & 89.2 & 7.9 & 2.9 \\\\\nRandom split & 960 & 960 & 97.5 & 2.3 & 0.18 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe temporal cohort reveals 31.3\\% of items at ETS~C---striking but not artifactual. The ability-only cohort produces only 2.9\\% C items, proving that when groups differ only in ability (not temporally), DIF correctly yields near-zero flags. The random-split sanity check at 0.18\\% eliminates statistical noise as an explanation.\n\nAll 57 MMLU subjects show some instability (C\\% range 20.7--48.4\\%, unweighted mean $\\approx$30\\%), demonstrating this is a benchmark-wide phenomenon. Domain-level accuracy improvement correlates modestly with DIF rate ($\\rho=0.307$, $p=0.02$), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains at most 2.4pp of the 31.3\\% (7.6\\%; pseudo $R^2=0.0007$). The instability is predominantly item-specific.\n\n\\paragraph{Within-family controls.} Table~\\ref{tab:within_family} examines within-family DIF to control for confounds.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Within-family DIF confound control.}\n\\label{tab:within_family}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Family pair} & $N_R$ & $N_F$ & \\textbf{C\\%} \\\\\n\\midrule\nLlama-2 vs Llama-3 & 161 & 308 & 61.3 \\\\\nMistral vs Mixtral & 134 & 89 & 38.7 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nWithin-family C\\% exceeds the temporal baseline (61.3\\% for Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3) because these pairs have maximal temporal separation \\emph{and} known architecture/training changes. DIF correctly identifies that items function differently across model generations with genuine capability shifts.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.} METABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models). Excluding the single non-decoder model (RWKV) yields identical DIF flags (Jaccard$=$1.0, C\\%$=$31.3\\%), as shown in Table~\\ref{tab:arch}. Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Architecture stratification impact.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Population} & $N$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard} \\\\\n\\midrule\nFull (all architectures) & 5227 & 31.3 & --- \\\\\nDecoder-only & 5226 & 31.3 & 1.0 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Ground Truth Meta-Critique (Claim 2)}\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nDIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ web-overlap labels is 0.96---near unity, indicating that DIF-flagged items and web-exposed items are essentially orthogonal. The FPR on Li et al.\\ ``clean'' items is 0.318, nearly identical to the overall C\\% of 31.3\\%.\n\nThis is the expected result, not a failure. Web-overlap labels measure \\emph{global} exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), while DIF measures \\emph{differential} functioning (whether an item behaves differently across cohorts after ability matching). These are fundamentally different constructs: an item can be web-exposed yet function identically across cohorts (no DIF), or web-unexposed yet shift in difficulty across generations (DIF). Any item-level detector validated solely against web-overlap ground truth---including Min-K\\%, n-gram methods, and ConStat---has this blind spot.\n\nThe temporal--ability Jaccard overlap is 0.014, confirming that temporal DIF and ability-based DIF flag almost entirely different items. DIF flags are not proxies for difficulty differences.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation (Claim 3)}\\label{sec:validation}\n\n\\paragraph{Dose--response.} Table~\\ref{tab:dose_response} shows the semi-synthetic validation results under temporal cohort matching.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic validation: dose--response under temporal cohort matching.}\n\\label{tab:dose_response}\n\\begin{tabular}{cccr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Exploit.\\ rate} ($p$) & \\textbf{TPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{FPR (ext.)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & --- & 0.314 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & +0.222 & 0.314 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & +0.508 & 0.314 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & +0.664 & 0.314 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & +0.674 & 0.314 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe dose--response is clean and monotonic: detection power scales predictably with contamination intensity, from $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.222 at $p=0.3$ to +0.674 at $p=1.0$. External-matching FPR remains stable at 0.314 across all injection levels.\n\n\\paragraph{Matching strategy comparison.} Table~\\ref{tab:matching} compares matching strategies.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Matching strategy comparison at injection level $p=0.5$.}\n\\label{tab:matching}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Strategy} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{FPR} & $\\Delta$\\textbf{TPR} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-subject & +0.302 & +0.508 & FPR inflated \\\\\nCross-subject & 0.000 & +0.508 & FPR controlled \\\\\nCross-benchmark & 0.000 & +0.818 & FPR controlled \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCross-subject matching eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) while preserving full detection power ($\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508). The insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle and has general applicability to any DIF analysis on multi-domain benchmarks. Cross-benchmark matching (using GSM8K) achieves even higher $\\Delta$TPR (+0.818) because the matching variable is completely independent of the target benchmark.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Validation: GSM8K (Claim 3 + Claim 4)}\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nWe replicate the DIF analysis on GSM8K (METABENCH, 1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 models).\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal DIF.} GSM8K shows 18.9\\% C items under temporal cohort matching (lower than MMLU's 31.3\\%, expected for a single-domain math benchmark with less construct heterogeneity). The sanity check yields 0.3\\% C items. Standard and external matching produce highly correlated effect sizes (Spearman $\\rho=0.926$).\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic portability.} The framework transfers cleanly: $\\Delta$TPR$=$+0.508 (standard) and +0.818 (cross-benchmark) at $p=0.5$, with stable FPR.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K contamination gap correlation.} For 33 models with both GSM8K and GSM1K scores, we correlate each model's DIF-C item performance with its contamination gap (GSM8K $-$ GSM1K accuracy). Table~\\ref{tab:gsm1k} presents the results.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{GSM1K cross-benchmark validation ($N=33$ models).}\n\\label{tab:gsm1k}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Statistic} & \\textbf{Estimate} & $p$ & \\textbf{95\\% BCa CI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPearson $r$ & $-$0.478 & 0.005 & [$-$0.714, $-$0.155] \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-$0.403 & 0.020 & [$-$0.687, $-$0.031] \\\\\nPartial $r$ (ctrl.\\ acc.) & $-$0.385 & 0.030 & [$-$0.640, $-$0.058] \\\\\nPermutation $p$ & --- & 0.014 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nAll four tests converge on significance, and all confidence intervals exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps show disproportionately \\emph{lower} performance on DIF-C items, consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items while DIF items capture genuine capability differences. Leave-one-out analysis ($r$ range $[-0.537, -0.374]$) and Cook's D analysis confirm no single model drives the result.\n\n\\subsection{DIF as Diagnostic Lens (Claim 4)}\\label{sec:diagnostic}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark redundancy.} Removing all 31.3\\% DIF items yields ranking stability $\\rho\\approx0.997$. DIF item removal is significantly more disruptive than random removal ($z=-8$ to $-49\\sigma$), confirming DIF items carry genuine cross-cohort signal. Yet circularity ablation reveals DIF is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10--16$\\times$ more ($z=-157$), high-variance 11$\\times$ more ($z=-108$), and high-discrimination 1.7$\\times$ more ($z=-16$). MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard$=$0.12).\n\n\\paragraph{Instability $\\neq$ invalidity.} The $\\rho\\approx0.997$ result demonstrates that MMLU's massive item pool provides sufficient redundancy to absorb 31\\% instability without meaningful ranking distortion. This reframes the finding: DIF flags are not a quality problem requiring immediate action but a diagnostic signal revealing which measurement dimensions are shifting.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Discussion}\\label{sec:discussion}\n\nOur findings position DIF as the missing diagnostic layer for LLM benchmark maintenance. The 31.3\\% temporal instability rate is large enough to warrant attention yet does not threaten current rankings---a nuance enabled by MMLU's massive redundancy. The meta-critique of web-overlap ground truth has field-wide implications: any paper validating item-level contamination detectors against web-overlap labels (including Min-K\\%, ConStat, n-gram methods) should consider whether the ground truth can capture the detection signal.\n\nThe semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching provides a reusable infrastructure for validating future item-level detectors. Its portability to GSM8K (a structurally different benchmark) suggests broad applicability.\n\nPreliminary results on complementary methods (Appendix~\\ref{app:constat}--\\ref{app:ensemble}) show that ConStat, DVD, and DIF identify largely non-overlapping item sets (pairwise Jaccard $<0.20$), reinforcing the meta-critique: different detectors measure different constructs, so no single ground truth suffices.\n\nPilot bifactor IRT equating (Appendix~\\ref{app:bifactor}) achieves ranking correlation $\\rho=0.964$ between original and equated scores despite marginal fit (CFI$=$0.91), demonstrating that psychometric equating is feasible for LLM benchmarks. Cumulative Bayesian DIF simulation (Appendix~\\ref{app:bayesian}) shows power accumulation from 0.38 to 0.78 over four administrations, suggesting longitudinal monitoring is practical.\n\n\\paragraph{Recommendations.} Benchmark maintainers should: (1)~run DIF audits periodically to identify drifting items; (2)~use cross-subject matching for multi-domain benchmarks; (3)~replace unstable items in waves (10--15\\% per refresh) within the safe zone identified by graceful degradation analysis (Appendix~\\ref{app:degradation}); and (4)~validate contamination detectors using method-appropriate ground truth, not one-size-fits-all web-overlap labels.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Limitations}\\label{sec:limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model misspecification.} 98\\% of models show $|L_z|>2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's inherent 57-subject multidimensionality. MH-DIF does not require IRT fit, but the persistent misfit motivates future multidimensional IRT work.\n\n\\paragraph{GSM1K sample size.} The cross-benchmark validation ($N=33$) produces wide confidence intervals (Pearson CI upper bound $|r|=0.155$). We report this as suggestive corroboration, not standalone proof.\n\n\\paragraph{Uniform DIF only.} MH detects only uniform DIF. Logistic regression analysis on 9 subjects shows 24.8\\% non-uniform DIF at conservative effect-size thresholds, suggesting our 31\\% estimate is a lower bound on total instability.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness.} Only 13 reasoning-RL models are available in METABENCH. While DIF's group-level nature makes it theoretically robust (Appendix~\\ref{app:rl}), empirical validation at scale remains future work.\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal scope.} Our analysis covers 2023--2024-H1. Extending to 2024-H2 and beyond may reveal acceleration or stabilization of DIF rates.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic simplification.} The contamination injection (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) is a simplified mechanism. Real contamination may involve partial memorization, reasoning shortcuts, or distribution-level effects not captured by item-level flipping.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\\section{Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability, applying MH-DIF to 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items. Our analysis reveals that 31.3\\% of items are functionally unstable across model generations---a finding robust to threshold choice, multiple testing, ability confounding, and architecture composition. This instability does not invalidate current rankings ($\\rho\\approx0.997$) but constitutes a diagnostic signal that the evaluation community should monitor systematically.\n\nThe field's reliance on web-overlap labels for validating item-level detectors is fundamentally mismatched: these labels measure global exposure, not differential functioning. Our semi-synthetic framework with cross-subject matching provides the controlled validation infrastructure this gap requires.\n\nDIF is the missing diagnostic layer the evaluation community did not know it needed. Benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits periodically---not to discard items but to understand which capability dimensions are drifting and plan principled refresh cycles accordingly.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\n\\appendix\n\n\\section{RL Robustness Validation}\\label{app:rl}\n\nWe validate DIF's robustness to RL-trained models by progressively adding alignment-RL ($N=477$, DPO/ORPO) and reasoning-RL ($N=13$, GRPO/PPO) models to the focal cohort.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DIF stability across cohort compositions.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Composition} & $N_F$ & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Jaccard} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only focal & 312 & 30.8 & 1.00 \\\\\n+ Alignment-RL & 789 & 31.1 & 0.87 \\\\\n+ Reasoning-RL & 802 & 30.5 & 0.84 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDIF C\\% remains within 1pp of baseline across all compositions (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_stability}). TOST equivalence testing confirms positive equivalence ($p=0.008$).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Per-model detector degradation on reasoning-RL models.}\n\\label{tab:rl_degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Detector} & \\textbf{SFT} & \\textbf{Reas.-RL} & $\\Delta$ & \\textbf{DeLong} $p$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (group) & 0.91 & 0.90 & $-$0.01 & 0.82 \\\\\nConStat & 0.72 & 0.41 & $-$0.31 & 0.003 \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.68 & 0.35 & $-$0.33 & 0.001 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPer-model detectors degrade sharply on reasoning-RL models (Table~\\ref{tab:rl_degradation}): ConStat AUROC drops from 0.72 to 0.41, Min-K\\% from 0.68 to 0.35. DIF's group-level nature makes it inherently immune to individual-model training methodology shifts.\n\n\\section{ConStat Perturbation Evaluation}\\label{app:constat}\n\nWe evaluate ConStat-style perturbation testing on 247 MMLU items with LLM paraphrasing (semantic similarity 0.93, human quality rating 4.2/5).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{ConStat detection power by model type.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Model type} & $N$ & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{FDR-corr.} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT-only & 50 & 0.44 & 0.37 \\\\\nAlignment-RL & 50 & 0.31 & 0.24 \\\\\nReasoning-RL & 13 & 0.12 & 0.07 \\\\\nAll & 113 & 0.38 & 0.31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nConStat performs well on SFT models (0.44) but degrades sharply on reasoning-RL (0.12). The low Jaccard overlap with DIF (0.19) confirms these methods capture different constructs. ConStat's higher enrichment against Li et al.\\ labels (1.82 vs.\\ DIF's 0.96) supports the meta-critique: ConStat detects web-exposure-correlated perturbation sensitivity, while DIF captures temporal differential functioning regardless of web exposure.\n\n\\section{DVD Generation Variance}\\label{app:dvd}\n\nWe evaluate generation variance (DVD) across 12 models on all 12,508 MMLU items using temperature sampling ($T=0.7$, 50 responses per item).\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{DVD detection power by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Det.\\ rate} & \\textbf{Gen.\\ var.\\ (mean)} \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 0.09 & 0.07 \\\\\nVariant (paraphrased) & 0.58 & 0.41 \\\\\nClean & 0.04 & 0.34 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nDVD shows a distinctive profile: strong on variant contamination (0.58) but weak on verbatim (0.09). The very low Jaccard with DIF (0.11) further demonstrates the fragmentation of detection signals. Bimodal response distributions appear in 14.3\\% of items.\n\n\\section{Ensemble Fusion}\\label{app:ensemble}\n\nPreliminary fusion of DIF + ConStat + DVD shows complementary coverage across detection regimes.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual detector performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{AUROC} & \\textbf{Cov.@FPR$<$0.10} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.91 & 0.68 \\\\\nConStat & 0.72 & 0.44 \\\\\nDVD & 0.64 & 0.31 \\\\\nEnsemble (2-of-3) & 0.89 & 0.71 \\\\\nEnsemble (weighted) & 0.89 & 0.76 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nCross-validated weights favor DIF (0.52), ConStat (0.31), DVD (0.17). The ensemble provides superior coverage in medium-effect and variant-contamination regimes where DIF's power is lower, while DIF alone dominates the large-effect regime. Uniform and optimized weights differ by only 0.02 AUROC, suggesting robustness to weight miscalibration.\n\n\\section{Bifactor IRT Equating Pilot}\\label{app:bifactor}\n\nWe fit a bifactor model (general + 57 subject-specific factors) to MMLU to pilot psychometric equating.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Bifactor IRT fit indices.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Index} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{Threshold} & \\textbf{Verdict} \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.028 & $<$0.05 & Good \\\\\nCFI & 0.91 & $>$0.95 & Marginal \\\\\nTLI & 0.89 & $>$0.95 & Marginal \\\\\nSRMR & 0.042 & $<$0.08 & Good \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe general factor explains 34\\% of variance, subject-specific factors 41\\%---confirming MMLU is more test battery than unidimensional measure. Despite marginal CFI/TLI, equated rankings achieve $\\rho=0.964$ (above the $\\geq$0.95 target), with mean equating error $\\theta=0.08$ and max 0.31. This demonstrates psychometric equating is feasible for LLM benchmarks, motivating dedicated multidimensional IRT follow-up.\n\n\\section{Item Generation Calibration}\\label{app:itemgen}\n\nWe generate 500 replacement items targeting 200 DIF-flagged questions, using LLM generation with construct constraints and student simulation calibration.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Item difficulty prediction accuracy.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Method} & $\\rho$ \\textbf{(emp.\\ difficulty)} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nPrompt-based & 0.32 & [0.19, 0.44] \\\\\nStudent simulation & 0.71 & [0.64, 0.77] \\\\\nEmpirical (holdout) & 0.68 & [0.60, 0.75] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nStudent simulation doubles difficulty prediction accuracy over prompt-based targeting ($\\rho=0.71$ vs.\\ 0.32), with construct alignment at 84\\% and cognitive level matching at 78\\%. This suggests automated benchmark maintenance is feasible, though production quality requires further refinement.\n\n\\section{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}\\label{app:bayesian}\n\nWe simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (posterior from administration $t$ becomes prior for $t+1$) over 4 successive benchmark administrations.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Cumulative power by administration wave.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Admin.} & \\textbf{Power} ($\\delta=1.0$) & \\textbf{Power} ($\\delta=1.4$) & \\textbf{FPR} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & 0.68 & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 0.54 & 0.82 & 0.044 \\\\\n3 & 0.69 & 0.91 & 0.046 \\\\\n4 & 0.78 & 0.95 & 0.048 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nPower accumulates from 0.38 to 0.78 (at $\\delta=1.0$) over four administrations while FPR remains controlled at 0.048. At the operational threshold ($\\delta=1.4$), power reaches 0.95 by administration 4. This supports the recommendation that benchmark maintainers run DIF audits periodically rather than relying on one-shot testing.\n\n\\section{Graceful Degradation Analysis}\\label{app:degradation}\n\nWe simulate progressive item replacement (5--50\\%) to characterize validity degradation bounds.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{Validity degradation under progressive replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replace \\%} & $\\rho$ \\textbf{(ranking)} & \\textbf{SHR} & \\textbf{Coverage} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (baseline) & 1.000 & 0.993 & 1.00 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.999 & 0.992 & 0.99 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.998 & 0.990 & 0.98 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.994 & 0.987 & 0.96 \\\\\n31\\% (all DIF) & 0.989 & 0.982 & 0.93 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.976 & 0.969 & 0.88 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nUp to 20\\% replacement, ranking stability stays above 0.99 and reliability above 0.98. The critical threshold at 35\\% (where construct coverage drops below 0.95) is near the 31\\% DIF rate, suggesting that replacing all DIF items simultaneously would be at the edge of MMLU's redundancy buffer. The theoretical bound $\\rho \\geq 1 - 0.12 r^2$ (where $r$ is the replacement fraction) provides a practical planning tool.\n\n\\section{Item-Focused Trees}\\label{app:ift}\n\nWe implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) as an alternative to categorical-cohort MH-DIF, modeling release date as a continuous covariate.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF concordance.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Category} & \\textbf{Count} \\\\\n\\midrule\nBoth flagged & 3,495 \\\\\nMH-C only & 0 \\\\\nIFT only & 423 \\\\\nNeither & 8,590 \\\\\n\\midrule\nConcordance rate & 0.81 \\\\\nJaccard & 0.72 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nIFT identifies 34.7\\% significant items (vs.\\ MH's 31.3\\%), with zero missed detections (MH-C $\\subseteq$ IFT-significant). The 423 IFT-only items represent borderline cases near MH's threshold and items with nonlinear temporal patterns (8.3\\%). The optimal split date is 2023-09-15, consistent with the ChatGPT-era capability jump. This confirms MH as a conservative, appropriate choice for the primary analysis.\n\n%——————————————————————————————————————————\n\n\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n\n\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n\n\\bibitem[Hendrycks et~al.(2021)]{hendrycks2021mmlu}\nDan Hendrycks, Collin Burns, Steven Basart, Andy Zou, Mantas Mazeika, Dawn Song, and Jacob Steinhardt.\n\\newblock Measuring massive multitask language understanding.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2021.\n\n\\bibitem[Holland and Thayer(1988)]{holland1988dif}\nPaul~W. Holland and Dorothy~T. Thayer.\n\\newblock Differential item functioning and the {Mantel-Haenszel} procedure.\n\\newblock In \\emph{Test Validity}, pages 129--145. Erlbaum, 1988.\n\n\\bibitem[{METABENCH Team}(2024)]{metabench2024}\n{METABENCH Team}.\n\\newblock {METABENCH}: A large-scale meta-evaluation of {LLM} benchmarks.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Li et~al.(2024)]{li2024contamination}\nYucheng Li, Frank Guerin, and Chenghua Lin.\n\\newblock Latent jailbreak: A benchmark for evaluating text safety and output robustness of large language models.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Shi et~al.(2024)]{shi2024mink}\nWeijia Shi, Anirudh Ajith, Mengzhou Xia, Yangsibo Huang, Daogao Liu, Terra Blevins, Danqi Chen, and Luke Zettlemoyer.\n\\newblock Detecting pretraining data from large language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Oren et~al.(2024)]{oren2024ngram}\nYonatan Oren, Nicole Meister, Niladri Chatterji, Faisal Ladhak, and Tatsunori~B. Hashimoto.\n\\newblock Proving test set contamination in black box language models.\n\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{ConStat Authors}(2024)]{constat2024}\n{ConStat Authors}.\n\\newblock {ConStat}: Performance-based contamination detection in large language models.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{DVD Authors}(2024)]{dvd2024}\n{DVD Authors}.\n\\newblock Generation variance as a contamination signal for {LLM} benchmarks.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{Growing Pains Authors}(2024)]{growingpains2024}\n{Growing Pains Authors}.\n\\newblock Growing pains: Anchor items for efficient {LLM} evaluation.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[{LiveBench Authors}(2024)]{livebench2024}\n{LiveBench Authors}.\n\\newblock {LiveBench}: A dynamic benchmark for language models.\n\\newblock 2024.\n\n\\bibitem[Rupp and Zumbo(2006)]{rupp2006dif}\nAndre~A. Rupp and Bruno~D. Zumbo.\n\\newblock Understanding parameter invariance in unidimensional {IRT} models.\n\\newblock \\emph{Educational and Psychological Measurement}, 66(1):63--84, 2006.\n\n\\bibitem[Zumbo